WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.219
&gt;&gt; So good evening or
 good afternoon. For those of
 you who don't know

00:00:08.220 --> 00:00:13.599
me, I'm Nicholas Negroponte.
 I'm one of the co-founders
 of the Media Lab.

00:00:13.600 --> 00:00:18.729
And I'm not really going
 to be the emcee But you'll
 see me come and go.

00:00:18.730 --> 00:00:22.759
And if people take too
 long, I might be pushed out
 to grab them off the

00:00:22.760 --> 00:00:29.909
stage. I was not only a
 colleague of Muriel's. I think it's
 fair to say she was

00:00:29.910 --> 00:00:36.139
my best friend. I've known
 her since the early 1960s
 and she had an

00:00:36.140 --> 00:00:41.899
enormous influence on me.
 And even though she
 was roughly 20 years

00:00:41.900 --> 00:00:48.229
older than I, we
 did things almost like brother
 and sister. We traveled

00:00:48.230 --> 00:00:52.649
together. We worked together.
 I was in the
 architecture department at one

00:00:52.650 --> 00:00:56.419
end of the building
 on Massachusetts Avenue and she
 was on the other

00:00:56.420 --> 00:01:01.289
end. And we really spent
 so much time together, I
 would say on average

00:01:01.290 --> 00:01:08.489
dinner twice a week.
 That's average. And partly
 that was because her

00:01:08.490 --> 00:01:13.709
house and MIT, if you drew
 a straight line, I was in
 the middle of it. So she

00:01:13.710 --> 00:01:21.289
would pick me up
 with this Chevrolet Impala that
 was infested with dog hair.

00:01:21.290 --> 00:01:22.939
 (Chuckles).

00:01:22.940 --> 00:01:25.389
&gt;&gt; I would get into
 garbage bags because I wore a
 suit and a tie in those

00:01:25.390 --> 00:01:28.849
days. And I
 would get into the car
 with my garbage bags.

00:01:28.850 --> 00:01:32.399
We would come -- she
 would drop me off at Building
 7. And she would go

00:01:32.400 --> 00:01:36.589
to the MIT Press. And
 then when the Media Lab
 got created on the Visible

00:01:36.590 --> 00:01:43.149
Language Workshop, which you'll
 hear a lot about,
 became very much part

00:01:43.150 --> 00:01:50.169
of the initial groups that
 sort of formed the Media
 Lab And Muriel worked

00:01:50.170 --> 00:01:56.059
here as one of
 our most colorful and
 important faculty members. Now,

00:01:56.060 --> 00:02:01.839
there are two Muriels. There's
 a Muriel that we know
 from her work. And

00:02:01.840 --> 00:02:08.069
there are quite a few
 people in the room who didn't
 know her as a person.

00:02:08.070 --> 00:02:12.809
A, because you may be
 too young. Or you just
 didn't have the chance to

00:02:12.810 --> 00:02:19.099
know Muriel. And the Muriel
 that we see in her
 work is crisp. It's incisive.

00:02:19.100 --> 00:02:26.559
It's almost cartesian. It's
 maybe even Helvetica if you
 could use the word

00:02:26.560 --> 00:02:31.479
generally to describe something
 rather Swiss and precise.
 And the Muriel --

00:02:31.480 --> 00:02:39.569
the second Muriel, the
 persona, I guess the first
 word would be much

00:02:39.570 --> 00:02:45.869
more colorful. Less decisive.
 For those of you
 who remember going out

00:02:45.870 --> 00:02:50.249
for dinner with Muriel and
 how she ordered food at
 a restaurant, it drove

00:02:50.250 --> 00:02:57.189
waiters crazy. She would say,
 well, no, I'll have --
 well, can you make this

00:02:57.190 --> 00:03:00.419
without -- actually I would
 prefer that if it doesn't
 -- the succotash without

00:03:00.420 --> 00:03:06.009
the corn. And it was --
 it was a much -- it
 wasn't crisp. And so the two

00:03:06.010 --> 00:03:15.669
Muriels created an influence
 that just never left
 this laboratory. And I'm

00:03:15.670 --> 00:03:21.389
sure many people who didn't
 know the persona will by
 the end of today.

00:03:21.390 --> 00:03:27.279
Because a lot of the
 speakers knew Muriel very well.
 A lot of the

00:03:27.280 --> 00:03:33.609
participants also did. So
 the coffee break, everybody
 will have Muriel

00:03:33.610 --> 00:03:39.119
Cooper stories. And I'm not
 going to bore you with
 too many of these. But

00:03:39.120 --> 00:03:44.829
in the early days of
 the Media Lab, we had a
 lot of funding from the

00:03:44.830 --> 00:03:49.069
military. And there was a
 General who was in what
 I think was then called

00:03:49.070 --> 00:03:53.159
the Seventh Army, which was
 all of the Army in
 Europe, who came to the

00:03:53.160 --> 00:04:00.089
Media Lab and met Muriel.
 And when he meets Muriel,
 he says, how much

00:04:00.090 --> 00:04:04.679
do you know about theaters? And
 she said, I know a lot.
 I studied to be an

00:04:04.680 --> 00:04:10.999
actress. And he said, it's not
 that kind of theater. And he
 then sort of -- he

00:04:11.000 --> 00:04:15.499
says to -- these
 guys don't come alone. There
 are privates. There are

00:04:15.500 --> 00:04:20.419
people. He said, where is
 the men's room? And he
 said, a hand grenade's

00:04:20.420 --> 00:04:27.259
throw down the corridor,
 sir. Muriel used that expression
 for the next 10

00:04:27.260 --> 00:04:32.539
years. She would tell people,
 oh, you need the men's
 room? It's a hand

00:04:32.540 --> 00:04:37.269
grenade's throw down the
 corridor. (Chuckles) So that's
 the kind of person

00:04:37.270 --> 00:04:41.869
Muriel was. When Jerry
 Wiesner and I were raising
 money, this was before

00:04:41.870 --> 00:04:45.769
either building was built,
 he brought Avery Fischer
 around And he said,

00:04:45.770 --> 00:04:52.279
let's go introduce Avery
 Fischer. And he introduces
 Muriel. He says, Muriel,

00:04:52.280 --> 00:04:58.519
this is Avery Fischer. And
 Muriel says, oh, are you
 the fat cat? And Jerry

00:04:58.520 --> 00:05:03.339
wanted to die. He never
 for gave her. He quoted
 the story over and over

00:05:03.340 --> 00:05:09.439
again. But he always did it
 with a lot of laughs. So
 I think today you'll hear --

00:05:09.440 --> 00:05:13.439
if you don't hear explicit
 stories, you're going to hear
 about a person who

00:05:13.440 --> 00:05:19.949
really made the Media
 Lab different. We pride
 ourself on being different.

00:05:19.950 --> 00:05:26.079
But boy, Muriel was very,
 very different. So in keeping
 with that, when we

00:05:26.080 --> 00:05:30.869
did this event, or when
 we were starting to think
 of this event, it's almost

00:05:30.870 --> 00:05:37.859
the case that the people
 who aren't here are dead.
 That was the only

00:05:37.860 --> 00:05:42.989
excuse. Almost everybody else
 came. And the --
 really. It's almost 100%

00:05:42.990 --> 00:05:48.799
showing in terms of the
 -- who was invited and
 who actually got here. And

00:05:48.800 --> 00:05:53.579
then when we asked people
 to help with the graphics
 and people to help

00:05:53.580 --> 00:06:00.609
do the event, we go
 to our usual port of call,
 the gold standard of graphics,

00:06:00.610 --> 00:06:04.459
we went to Pentagram.
 Now, we go to Pentagram
 for everything. So going

00:06:04.460 --> 00:06:10.769
to Pentagram wasn't that
 special. But Pentagram, thanks
 to Aron -- thanks

00:06:10.770 --> 00:06:17.089
to Aron Vinegar, Aron FAy, Michael
 Bierut and I don't know if
 I know -- if I

00:06:17.090 --> 00:06:19.809
met Daisy
 Lee. Are
 you here?

00:06:19.810 --> 00:06:22.809
&gt;&gt; (Inaudible).

00:06:22.810 --> 00:06:25.679
&gt;&gt; She does all of
 the work. She's not even here.
 But you guys, you know,

00:06:25.680 --> 00:06:31.849
you hit it out of the
 box. And I know exactly why.
 And we're going to see

00:06:31.850 --> 00:06:38.469
this again over the afternoon.
 So I don't have to
 introduce the next speaker.

00:06:38.470 --> 00:06:45.189
Muriel will. In a video
 that I believe we'll roll
 in a second. Thank you.

00:06:45.190 --> 00:06:48.319
 (Applause).

00:06:48.320 --> 00:06:52.489
&gt;&gt; Muriel was with MIT
 Press for quite a while. She's
 been with the Media Lab

00:06:52.490 --> 00:06:57.769
for quite a bit.
 She's always been two
 or three steps ahead

00:06:57.770 --> 00:07:01.119
of me
 with everything she did.
 And it's an honor

00:07:01.120 --> 00:07:07.289
and a pleasure
 to have Muriel join
 us today thanking you.

00:07:07.290 --> 00:07:09.389
 (Applause).

00:07:09.390 --> 00:07:12.749
&gt;&gt; I have a
 confession. And I hope that
 everybody in the audience

00:07:12.750 --> 00:07:16.429
will forgive me. In
 those early days when
 we were both very

00:07:16.430 --> 00:07:26.449
young and terrified, I made
 the mistake -- I remember
 how I did it. I

00:07:26.450 --> 00:07:31.469
remember when I did it.
 And I think I remember where
 I did it. But I'm

00:07:31.470 --> 00:07:36.719
damned if I know
 why I did it. I
 introduced Nicky to Ricky.

00:07:36.720 --> 00:07:42.979
 (Chuckles)

00:07:42.980 --> 00:07:48.159
 (Applause)

00:07:48.160 --> 00:07:52.239
&gt;&gt; Can you -- I know you're
filming this or doing something.
But turn the lights

00:07:52.240 --> 00:07:56.079
down so I can
 see the audience, please, please
 just turn them down.

00:07:56.080 --> 00:07:58.669
Somebody has a switch for
 the lights and I know you
 don't want to do it.

00:07:58.670 --> 00:08:02.199
But it will be fine.
 Just turn them down, please,
 so I can see

00:08:02.200 --> 00:08:06.159
the audience. Those lights that
 are in my face. That's
 just better. Oh, yes. I

00:08:06.160 --> 00:08:11.019
have to do that every
 time you give a speech.
 You can't see the audience

00:08:11.020 --> 00:08:17.119
And if you can't see
 a face, how can you give
 a chat? Well, my opening

00:08:17.120 --> 00:08:22.159
lines were not Nicky to Ricky.
 But I was going to say
 that Nicholas -- I was

00:08:22.160 --> 00:08:32.909
introduced to Nicholas by
 Muriel. She changed my
 life. Lives have

00:08:32.910 --> 00:08:39.869
moments. Lives are not
 continuums. You might look
 back and think they

00:08:39.870 --> 00:08:43.089
are continuums. But no, they
 are just moments. And forget
 a lot of the shit

00:08:43.090 --> 00:08:48.119
between the moments. But
 the moments, they really
 change you. And it's

00:08:48.120 --> 00:08:54.009
not that you should forget
 the stuff between it. But
 it's really just stuff. A

00:08:54.010 --> 00:09:02.469
lot of stuff. Like
 George Carlin used to talk
 about stuff. Anyway, she

00:09:02.470 --> 00:09:07.129
introduced me to Nicholas
 when he had the
 Architecture Machine And

00:09:07.130 --> 00:09:17.699
that was a change in my life. And
her wanting to introduce me to

00:09:17.700 --> 00:09:27.339
somebody else who she felt
 was very important was a
 change in my life.

00:09:27.340 --> 00:09:32.859
Recently people have
 been telling me that
 I'm abrasively charming.

00:09:32.860 --> 00:09:34.219
 (Chuckles).

00:09:34.220 --> 00:09:38.019
&gt;&gt; And there's a certain
 truth in both of those
 things that I am abrasive

00:09:38.020 --> 00:09:42.459
and I am
 charming. I know you're not
 supposed to say either

00:09:42.460 --> 00:09:46.219
about yourself. But it's
 perfectly clear what I'm saying.
 And if you're clear,

00:09:46.220 --> 00:09:51.019
that beats every other thing.
 Just being clear is enough.
 And so I could

00:09:51.020 --> 00:09:59.629
never get any -- I
 wouldn't have gotten the media
 -- the MIT Press or

00:09:59.630 --> 00:10:03.959
anybody else to publish any
 of my little books. And
 I had been doing

00:10:03.960 --> 00:10:07.249
books for a while. I just
 have an idea and I do a
 little book And I don't have

00:10:07.250 --> 00:10:14.349
a publisher. I spit it
 out But she indulged me
 and got them to do

00:10:14.350 --> 00:10:19.519
exceptions about a whole bunch
 of little booklets that I
 did over the years.

00:10:19.520 --> 00:10:24.539
And they really -- they
 really -- she really changed
 my life. I did something

00:10:24.540 --> 00:10:30.639
for her. But I didn't know
 I was doing it. And she
 didn't know I was doing it.

00:10:30.640 --> 00:10:35.919
But in the last year of
 her life, I finally got to
 her -- and she's everything that

00:10:35.920 --> 00:10:46.849
Nicholas said. The dogs,
 the car. Her taking off
 her shoes, walking around

00:10:46.850 --> 00:10:50.769
in her socks. I mean, it's
 just -- but she didn't know
 she was doing it. It was

00:10:50.770 --> 00:10:55.669
just what she was. If I
 -- I used to dress funny when
 I went to Penn but I

00:10:55.670 --> 00:10:59.749
knew purposely I was
 trying to get attention. She
 wasn't trying to get

00:10:59.750 --> 00:11:02.829
attention. She was just
 being herself. Just about as
 genuine as she could

00:11:02.830 --> 00:11:09.419
be. That was just
 her. I was always more
 phony than she was.

00:11:09.420 --> 00:11:13.149
 (Chuckles).

00:11:13.150 --> 00:11:17.339
&gt;&gt; I came
 and saw her stuff here
 a number of times.

00:11:17.340 --> 00:11:23.709
By the way, this is
 -- I said it to Juan
 Enriquez as I came in.

00:11:23.710 --> 00:11:26.839
We had a quick
 conversation. And I had
 never said that before.

00:11:26.840 --> 00:11:30.229
But I realized
 that it's something
 I should repeat

00:11:30.230 --> 00:11:36.959
This is the greatest
 real school in the
 world. There's some woowoo

00:11:36.960 --> 00:11:41.219
schools that are probably
 nice where little kids count
 beads and they do

00:11:41.220 --> 00:11:45.279
things and they think they
 learn to be nice people
 and all of those things.

00:11:45.280 --> 00:11:48.289
There's a lot of
 little woowoo schools with
 different things and

00:11:48.290 --> 00:11:53.469
experimental schools. But
 this school is extraordinary.
 We, what Nicholas

00:11:53.470 --> 00:11:58.189
has done, others following
 Nicholas, paralleling Nicholas, the
 effect he has

00:11:58.190 --> 00:12:02.769
had. I walked around. I
 was early because I had
 nothing to do. And I

00:12:02.770 --> 00:12:09.889
walked around the school.
 And my goodness. It
 really is Disneyland for

00:12:09.890 --> 00:12:19.649
grownups. It's a dream. It's
 the waking dream. And so
 this is a great place

00:12:19.650 --> 00:12:27.339
that we're all sitting
 in here. And great places
 attract great people. And

00:12:27.340 --> 00:12:33.649
Muriel was a great person.
 She was just simply a
 great person. And I

00:12:33.650 --> 00:12:37.319
finally convinced her -- I
 started a conference I ran
 for a while called TED.

00:12:37.320 --> 00:12:41.919
And I finally convinced her
 to come to one of
 the fifth TED Conferences.

00:12:41.920 --> 00:12:49.429
And she came with David
 Small, who is in the
 audience here. They came

00:12:49.430 --> 00:12:53.869
and I had her scheduled
 I think for the second
 day. I don't remember when

00:12:53.870 --> 00:12:55.869
you were scheduled David. I
 think it was the second
 day. It might have

00:12:55.870 --> 00:12:59.359
been the first. And she
 came with David. And they
 had nothing. They had

00:12:59.360 --> 00:13:02.979
nothing to show. They hadn't
 worked on anything. They just
 came in. And I

00:13:02.980 --> 00:13:09.829
was just depressed about that.
 And so I changed the --
 I could do what I

00:13:09.830 --> 00:13:12.759
wanted. I changed the program.
 And I think they were
 on the fourth day.

00:13:12.760 --> 00:13:16.509
And then they stayed
 up for three days.
 And they developed their

00:13:16.510 --> 00:13:22.839
presentation. It was a
 charrette. Beyond charrettes. It
 was amazing. I told

00:13:22.840 --> 00:13:26.359
David if I say
 something that at least makes
 the story understandable not

00:13:26.360 --> 00:13:30.799
to stand up. If I
 say something that really disturbs
 him and is not factual

00:13:30.800 --> 00:13:34.429
that he should stand up
 and stop me. I'm just telling
 you my memory of it.

00:13:34.430 --> 00:13:44.479
And then they did
 this amazing presentation. And it
 was live. They had

00:13:44.480 --> 00:13:51.509
computers all over. This is
 a long time ago. This
 is just a long time --

00:13:51.510 --> 00:13:54.179
it's when nothing worked

00:13:54.180 --> 00:13:56.179
 (Chuckles).

00:13:56.180 --> 00:13:58.699
&gt;&gt; I mean literally
 nothing worked. You knew
 that anybody who was doing

00:13:58.700 --> 00:14:02.459
a presentation with
 a computer it wasn't
 going to work.

00:14:02.460 --> 00:14:05.049
They would have to boot
 it up again. It was
 just the way it was.

00:14:05.050 --> 00:14:15.019
It was acceptable.
 And it was astonishing.
 It was just astonishing.

00:14:15.020 --> 00:14:27.409
And it was my dream.
 It was a dream. And
 I always stayed on

00:14:27.410 --> 00:14:31.679
stage. I think I left the
 stage to watch it that time.
 I always used to stay on

00:14:31.680 --> 00:14:35.109
stage so I could stay
 behind people to have them
 stop talking. And I left

00:14:35.110 --> 00:14:39.809
the stage and watched it.
 And I was transplanted. I
 didn't think you could

00:14:39.810 --> 00:14:46.879
do that. And I
 said, it was like flying
 through information. Flying through

00:14:46.880 --> 00:14:52.269
understanding. It was something
 new. We have gotten so
 used to it. We

00:14:52.270 --> 00:14:56.129
see it in all of
 the Goddamn ads now. We're
 always flying through letters

00:14:56.130 --> 00:15:00.189
and flying through things.
 But that was Muriel and
 her students. It was

00:15:00.190 --> 00:15:05.519
something that was broke
 through -- it was
 a break-through in thinking

00:15:05.520 --> 00:15:11.139
about computer graphics
 and content. Not computer
 graphics and just

00:15:11.140 --> 00:15:18.469
graphics and pretty. And
 flexing muscles. It was
 with content. Content and

00:15:18.470 --> 00:15:25.809
time. And it was three
 dimensional maps of time. It
 was all kind of aerial

00:15:25.810 --> 00:15:32.179
maps. It was the Cirque
 du Soleil with people with
 printouts on them. It

00:15:32.180 --> 00:15:42.079
was special. It was really
 special. I recorded -- I didn't
 record it. I can't even

00:15:42.080 --> 00:15:49.029
type. But I had people
 record things. And that little
video is, as far as I

00:15:49.030 --> 00:15:57.699
know, the only evidence that
 she and David and her
 students and her head

00:15:57.700 --> 00:16:02.299
and her time and her
 dog and her car and the
 -- you know and everything,

00:16:02.300 --> 00:16:09.559
put that together. And it's
 become an icon of a
 moment in history when

00:16:09.560 --> 00:16:14.129
some things came together.
 It's like the photograph
 that Watson and Crick

00:16:14.130 --> 00:16:18.699
used to build their
 Tinkertoy model. It's a moment
 in time that put

00:16:18.700 --> 00:16:25.889
something together that we
 can stamp when people
 started thinking that

00:16:25.890 --> 00:16:30.119
way. And it allowed
 people to think differently.
Because the tool allows you

00:16:30.120 --> 00:16:34.849
to think of things you
 couldn't think of, except for
 the first person who

00:16:34.850 --> 00:16:42.109
does that. Michelangelo stands in
 front of a huge piece
 of stone. And he

00:16:42.110 --> 00:16:47.459
wouldn't be able to
 do anything about it until
 somebody had invented, prior

00:16:47.460 --> 00:16:53.129
to that, a hammer and a
 chisel. And then they say, oh,
 I can let Moses out

00:16:53.130 --> 00:16:57.669
because he has a hammer
 and chisel. But he couldn't
 even think of doing

00:16:57.670 --> 00:17:02.169
that without -- he couldn't
 scrape him out with his
 fingernails. So her tools

00:17:02.170 --> 00:17:13.539
were these abilities to
 think of things that
 have changed our lives.

00:17:13.540 --> 00:17:17.589
Something came up maybe
 before this. And I
 forget the conversation. I

00:17:17.590 --> 00:17:21.059
forget who it was. But
 what came up is something
 I was thinking about a

00:17:21.060 --> 00:17:24.749
few weeks ago of how
 my life changed. A certain
 time when I was at

00:17:24.750 --> 00:17:30.399
Penn. I'm 82. So I
 can know how many years ago
 that was. And I was

00:17:30.400 --> 00:17:36.569
doing the student magazine
 then. There wasn't such
 a thing as offset

00:17:36.570 --> 00:17:40.399
printing except by
 an experimental printer in
 downtown Philadelphia, Gene

00:17:40.400 --> 00:17:45.409
Philbin at Falcon Press.
 But before everything was
 -- before that everything

00:17:45.410 --> 00:17:49.759
was done with the way
 you set type and doing
 that. So you couldn't even

00:17:49.760 --> 00:17:53.079
think of actually doing
 something else You couldn't think
 of doing the thing

00:17:53.080 --> 00:17:55.809
that Pentagram does. They
 wouldn't come into your
 head because you --

00:17:55.810 --> 00:18:01.309
they couldn't be actualized. So
 that -- they aren't the
 creation. But the tool

00:18:01.310 --> 00:18:07.119
allows you to think of
 things. And her -- she
 skipped the idea that without th

00:18:07.120 --> 00:18:13.049
e tool, she broke
 through and asked of
 our new inventions, do

00:18:13.050 --> 00:18:18.229
something that she was
 -- it was her waking
 dream. And we benefited

00:18:18.230 --> 00:18:23.759
from that. Well, I won't
 beat that dead horse There's
 a little light there. And

00:18:23.760 --> 00:18:27.699
I'm -- I don't know how
 much time I spoke I know
 I have 15 minutes. And

00:18:27.700 --> 00:18:31.279
this takes 6 minutes. And
 I realize I can't write better
 than I wrote then. It's

00:18:31.280 --> 00:18:35.739
just 6 minutes. And this
 was the -- I did
 a book called "Information

00:18:35.740 --> 00:18:41.329
Architects." And I dedicated
 it to Muriel many, many,
 many years ago. And

00:18:41.330 --> 00:18:45.589
this is what I read. I
 put that in the book. And this
 is what I read at the

00:18:45.590 --> 00:18:51.459
services for her after she
 died. And I was with her
 the day before she died,

00:18:51.460 --> 00:18:56.419
a day and a half
 before she died. She was at
 an AGI Conference over in

00:18:56.420 --> 00:19:02.979
Cambridge. And we were
 hanging with Bobbi Greenberg. She
 and I and a

00:19:02.980 --> 00:19:07.939
few others. And she
 had to get back home.
 Nicholas' memory is slightly

00:19:07.940 --> 00:19:11.369
different than mine. I
 don't know what's right.
 It doesn't make any

00:19:11.370 --> 00:19:14.705
difference what's right. She came
home and Nicholas asked her to

00:19:14.706 --> 00:19:18.399
introduce somebody and we know that
she passed away suddenly at that

00:19:18.400 --> 00:19:24.419
moment. But she showed twice
 the thing that you have
 all seen I think or

00:19:24.420 --> 00:19:27.649
it's around this show or
 they will show it today.
 I don't know what the

00:19:27.650 --> 00:19:32.699
program is today. But it was
 shown -- we saw it live
 at TED. It was shown

00:19:32.700 --> 00:19:36.539
one other time afterwards.
 And then the last time
 was at the AGI

00:19:36.540 --> 00:19:43.759
Conference in Cambridge. And
 it was remarkable. So
 I'm going to come

00:19:43.760 --> 00:19:46.209
down here and read that.
 I do have 6 minutes,
 whoever is keeping time?

00:19:46.210 --> 00:19:53.159
Do I still have 6
 minutes left? Say yes, please.
 Well, I'm coming here

00:19:53.160 --> 00:19:56.589
because I saw there
 was more light here. Somebody
 turned down those lights.

00:19:56.590 --> 00:20:04.399
 (Chuckles)

00:20:04.400 --> 00:20:08.059
&gt;&gt; Okay I'm not
 good at reading. I'm sorry.
 I'm better at talking.

00:20:08.060 --> 00:20:13.319
But this is good to
 read. I believe that Muriel has
 wings. We met. I was 29

00:20:13.320 --> 00:20:25.649
and she was 38.
 We last laughed and planned
 together on the day

00:20:25.650 --> 00:20:33.469
before she was to die I
 was 59 and she was 68. I
 knew her for 30 years. On

00:20:33.470 --> 00:20:37.709
my first meeting, I felt
 those wings as they wrapped
 around me like a

00:20:37.710 --> 00:20:43.619
mother hen. I was
 sharp tongued, arrogant, coming to
 the MIT Press trying

00:20:43.620 --> 00:20:48.689
to get my ideas published.
 And she walked me through
 the door after door

00:20:48.690 --> 00:20:53.849
that I couldn't myself navigate
 or open. I next saw
 the wings as they

00:20:53.850 --> 00:21:00.089
created a wind and showed
 me the wind of change.
 She introduced me to

00:21:00.090 --> 00:21:04.749
Nicholas. And the
 notion of dreams. Finding
 your way through

00:21:04.750 --> 00:21:10.759
information. A different kind
 of navigation. The next winds
 -- wings I saw

00:21:10.760 --> 00:21:18.209
her wear where the
 wings of a peace dove
 as she red-eyed and

00:21:18.210 --> 00:21:24.219
passionate, struggled to
 get McCullin's book "Is
 Anyone Taking Any

00:21:24.220 --> 00:21:32.519
Notice?" published. That poignant
 book about the Vietnam
 War and death.

00:21:32.520 --> 00:21:39.669
She wore wings on
 her many cardiographic exercises.
 Flying on airplane

00:21:39.670 --> 00:21:45.459
wings, flying in space
 over maps, over information.
 Watching as the aerial

00:21:45.460 --> 00:21:51.729
perspective changed with
 clouds refocusing her vision
 of a man-created

00:21:51.730 --> 00:21:57.939
reality. The wings of
 triumph and the aura of
 discovery were all around

00:21:57.940 --> 00:22:02.299
Muriel at the TED5
 conference She worked with David
 Small for three days

00:22:02.300 --> 00:22:07.099
to assemble together the
 premiere of a presentation,
 which she hesitantly

00:22:07.100 --> 00:22:12.649
at first, then with
 noticeable joy, showed to
 the extraordinary audience of

00:22:12.650 --> 00:22:18.389
that conference. It changed
 forever the visual paradigm
 of information for

00:22:18.390 --> 00:22:23.039
all who saw the presentation.
 As I came on stage
 holding back the tears

00:22:23.040 --> 00:22:28.999
of join that come
 when you have seen
 something absolutely magnificent, I

00:22:29.000 --> 00:22:37.789
said, Muriel, I believe we
 have all had dreams of
 flying. And here you

00:22:37.790 --> 00:22:43.259
allowed us to make
 those dreams a reality as
 we are flying through

00:22:43.260 --> 00:22:49.189
information. It was a
 real-time display of heavenly
 navigation. Later here at

00:22:49.190 --> 00:22:53.989
the Media Lab, she
 once again went through the
 presentation with Bill Mitchell

00:22:53.990 --> 00:22:58.339
and myself as bookends.
 And the three of
 us talking to Nicholas' class.

00:22:58.340 --> 00:23:03.229
And then three
 months later, having retitled
 the presentation Flying

00:23:03.230 --> 00:23:07.079
Through Information, she
 showed the videotape to
 the Alliance Graphique

00:23:07.080 --> 00:23:11.399
Internationale, AGI, at
 Cambridge, England. Henry Steiner,
 who is from

00:23:11.400 --> 00:23:18.209
Hong Kong and then
 President AGI, had seen it
 at TED. Bobbi Greenberg

00:23:18.210 --> 00:23:23.469
and I spoke with her
 of what was going to
 happen next and what the

00:23:23.470 --> 00:23:27.999
dreams of flying through information
 released in each of us.
 For me it was

00:23:28.000 --> 00:23:33.139
the ability to control
 one's personal understanding. That
 was the day

00:23:33.140 --> 00:23:41.229
before she died. I do
 believe Muriel has wings. They
 are wings that will

00:23:41.230 --> 00:23:45.729
encourage her students to
 be more of themselves.
 They will allow the

00:23:45.730 --> 00:23:49.839
people seeing her
 epiphanic and information structure
 to dream of

00:23:49.840 --> 00:23:55.109
understanding She was an
 architect of information. She
 now has the

00:23:55.110 --> 00:24:01.699
wings that few get in
 death. She will be in
 the waking dreams of those

00:24:01.700 --> 00:24:11.769
who are touched by
 the wind from her feathers
 of change and discovery.

00:24:11.770 --> 00:24:22.229
 (Applause).

00:24:22.230 --> 00:24:59.919
 [Music].

00:24:59.920 --> 00:25:08.049
&gt;&gt; Can you see
 me? Hello. Hello. There are
 two very important things that

00:25:08.050 --> 00:25:14.629
the Visible Language Workshop
 is looking at. One is
 the way in which graphics,

00:25:14.630 --> 00:25:20.589
which we define in
 the broadest sense, can
 be used to filter,

00:25:20.590 --> 00:25:25.969
define, qualify and
 edit that information. And
 secondly, what --

00:25:25.970 --> 00:25:31.939
the interface or the relationship
 -- the surface or the
 access of the person

00:25:31.940 --> 00:25:36.719
to the machine can be
 liked to promote the most
 creative and the most

00:25:36.720 --> 00:25:41.039
generative means of communication.
 And again if you
 look at the

00:25:41.040 --> 00:25:44.999
computer as an environment
 in which you do multiple
 tasks, and which is

00:25:45.000 --> 00:25:49.499
ubiquitous in your life,
 then it's even more
 important that this

00:25:49.500 --> 00:25:54.839
personalization and configurability
 take place. Because today
 I may want

00:25:54.840 --> 00:25:58.069
to work on
 music and then go
 to my cookbook.

00:25:58.070 --> 00:25:59.909
&gt;&gt; Right.

00:25:59.910 --> 00:26:03.379
&gt;&gt; And then
 read my newspaper. Or
 design my newspaper.

00:26:03.380 --> 00:26:05.789
&gt;&gt; Right.

00:26:05.790 --> 00:26:07.999
 [Music].

00:26:08.000 --> 00:26:12.499
&gt;&gt; Don't go
 away. Where are my notes?
 On the back of this.

00:26:12.500 --> 00:26:22.369
Now I've lost my fucking notes.
 Huh? (Chuckles) &gt;&gt; No. So -- oh,
 Jesus, here it is. Don't stop.

00:26:22.370 --> 00:26:31.269
Here we
 go. I'm
 ready.

00:26:31.270 --> 00:27:05.779
 [Music]

00:27:05.780 --> 00:27:09.209
 (Applause).

00:27:09.210 --> 00:27:14.619
&gt;&gt; Hi I'm
 Michael Bierut of Pentagram.
 We have assembled a

00:27:14.620 --> 00:27:18.419
panel to talk
 about Muriel as a
 designer. Like some of

00:27:18.420 --> 00:27:21.689
the other unlucky
 people here, I don't
 think I ever met Muriel.

00:27:21.690 --> 00:27:26.959
I sent her a
 letter in the late '70s
 that Rob Wiesenberger found

00:27:26.960 --> 00:27:33.109
and sent me a copy of
 that seems to indicate that I
 may have met her. I

00:27:33.110 --> 00:27:36.289
can't really -- I don't
 remember writing the letter and
 I don't remember what

00:27:36.290 --> 00:27:39.749
provoked me to write this
 letter. But I corresponded with
 her in some way.

00:27:39.750 --> 00:27:44.819
So at least we
 have that remote connection. But
 her influence, as Nicholas

00:27:44.820 --> 00:27:51.909
said, is so broad
 and so deep, that
 almost anyone practicing graphic

00:27:51.910 --> 00:27:57.159
design today would say
 that they have somehow
 met Muriel Cooper.

00:27:57.160 --> 00:28:01.259
We've got three people
 who can talk about this
 in more depth. Aron

00:28:01.260 --> 00:28:06.649
Vinegar, who is a professor
 at the University of Oslo
 and the author of

00:28:06.650 --> 00:28:13.539
several books, including "I
 Am a Monument," which is
 about Muriel and her

00:28:13.540 --> 00:28:17.709
work on "Learning from
 Las Vegas," the epical
 volume by Denise Scott

00:28:17.710 --> 00:28:23.979
Brown and Robert Venturi.
 Ben Fry, the creator
 of the programing

00:28:23.980 --> 00:28:28.899
environment
 called
 --

00:28:28.900 --> 00:28:32.219
 (Chuckles).

00:28:32.220 --> 00:28:34.369
&gt;&gt; Processing.

00:28:34.370 --> 00:28:39.069
&gt;&gt; -- Called Processing I'm
 sorry; you need a better,
 catchier name than that.

00:28:39.070 --> 00:28:42.009
&gt;&gt; Basic, something basic.

00:28:42.010 --> 00:28:44.139
 (Chuckles).

00:28:44.140 --> 00:28:48.599
&gt;&gt; He's a
 winner of the National Design
 Award from the Cooper-Hewitt Museum

00:28:48.600 --> 00:28:52.289
for Interactive
 Design, as is at
 least one other speaker

00:28:52.290 --> 00:28:56.579
here this evening.
 And finally, Ellen Lupton,
 the senior curator for

00:28:56.580 --> 00:29:01.329
design at the National
 Design Museum. And she's the
 person who sort of

00:29:01.330 --> 00:29:05.989
hands out those awards. And
 I will point out this
 very evening is the

00:29:05.990 --> 00:29:10.639
evening of the National
 Design Awards in New York
 City. Ellen is honor

00:29:10.640 --> 00:29:14.249
bound to be there but instead here.

00:29:14.250 --> 00:29:17.309
 (Chuckles).

00:29:17.310 --> 00:29:19.579
&gt;&gt; So that just
 shows where the priorities
 are when Muriel Cooper

00:29:19.580 --> 00:29:23.919
has something to
 say about them. So I
 would like to invite Ellen

00:29:23.920 --> 00:29:25.959
to talk
 about her and
 her legacy.

00:29:25.960 --> 00:29:27.749
&gt;&gt; That's great. I
 did get to meet
 Muriel. Very profound.

00:29:27.750 --> 00:29:31.239
And I was in
 the audience when Ricky
 didn't take his shoes off

00:29:31.240 --> 00:29:35.699
but she did and
 showed that amazing video. And
 I was trying to think,

00:29:35.700 --> 00:29:40.749
what would she have
 made of this today?
 All of you here,

00:29:40.750 --> 00:29:48.169
a giant book
 about her work. An
 honorary elevator button

00:29:48.170 --> 00:29:50.239
 (Chuckles).

00:29:50.240 --> 00:29:54.679
&gt;&gt; Her name
 type set in 35,000
 point purple Helvetica.

00:29:54.680 --> 00:29:57.709
I think she would have liked it.
(Chuckles)

00:29:57.710 --> 00:29:59.940
&gt;&gt; You know,
girls are supposed to be

00:30:00.000 --> 00:30:02.639
embarrassed by all of
 the attention. But I'll
 tell you a secret,

00:30:02.640 --> 00:30:05.809
we like it. It's good. It's good.

00:30:05.810 --> 00:30:08.039
 (Chuckles).

00:30:08.040 --> 00:30:14.279
&gt;&gt; And what would
 she have thought about the
 world of media today,

00:30:14.280 --> 00:30:18.619
this exploding,
 uncontrolled metastatic networked
 mass of

00:30:18.620 --> 00:30:27.719
digital communication? She said
 it all. She really
 knew what was happening.

00:30:27.720 --> 00:30:31.469
And it's been
 a great pleasure to think
 again about her work.

00:30:31.470 --> 00:30:36.979
And to read
 the wonderful essays about
 her voice and

00:30:36.980 --> 00:30:43.379
her students in the
 book that Rob and David
 put together. Muriel can't

00:30:43.380 --> 00:30:49.759
be here today. But I
 want her to be here. And
 so I pulled together some

00:30:49.760 --> 00:30:55.589
of her words. Because
 she needs to speak. And
 she was so articulate

00:30:55.590 --> 00:31:02.369
about the questions that
 obsess people like me and
 Michael. What is a

00:31:02.370 --> 00:31:08.179
book? What is an author?
 What is the role of
 graphic designers in all of

00:31:08.180 --> 00:31:14.169
that? What is the nature
 of communication? And what is
 it that we do,

00:31:14.170 --> 00:31:21.179
damn it, that adds value
 to communication? I pulled out
 a couple of her

00:31:21.180 --> 00:31:26.589
most stunning and important
 or famous books. But
 they are the books

00:31:26.590 --> 00:31:31.059
in which she had the
 most role as an author. And
 in her decades at MIT

00:31:31.060 --> 00:31:36.129
and her role at
 MIT Press, she was creating
 systems and ways that

00:31:36.130 --> 00:31:41.369
books could kind of
 generate themselves. But she
 also really got her

00:31:41.370 --> 00:31:46.419
fingers and hands and
 mind into certain books that
 very much bear her

00:31:46.420 --> 00:31:53.349
stamp as a co-author
 of information. She thought
 that books were like

00:31:53.350 --> 00:31:59.509
movies. She wanted to
 eliminate the gap between
 the designer and the

00:31:59.510 --> 00:32:04.509
process of printing and
 shaping information. So she
 played with offset

00:32:04.510 --> 00:32:10.129
as this kind of live
 medium, emotion medium. She used
 the grid in her

00:32:10.130 --> 00:32:17.999
amazing Bauhaus book as
 a way to give
 structure and architecture. But

00:32:18.000 --> 00:32:23.579
also to create constant
 change and surprise. My
 favorite part is the

00:32:23.580 --> 00:32:29.609
index. I'm sure she spent a
 lot of time on that index.
 And I think this is --

00:32:29.610 --> 00:32:37.589
this is actually the
 manuscript for the Bauhaus book.
 This is what we

00:32:37.590 --> 00:32:43.809
do. We take a pile
 of shit like that. These
 thousands of pages. That is

00:32:43.810 --> 00:32:49.969
not a book. It is
 not a book until someone
 comes through and gives it

00:32:49.970 --> 00:32:55.799
shape and makes it accessible
 to human beings to find
 their way in and

00:32:55.800 --> 00:33:02.209
out. And she talked
 about that. She talked about
 graphic design as a

00:33:02.210 --> 00:33:08.869
kind of filtering of
 this mass, this stream of
 information, the junk that

00:33:08.870 --> 00:33:14.779
authors hand us. And
 that we make into
 something coherent And she

00:33:14.780 --> 00:33:19.869
saw that the future
 would give readers and writers
 better ways to find

00:33:19.870 --> 00:33:26.429
their way through information,
 to give it a better
 shape. And she was

00:33:26.430 --> 00:33:31.669
interested in information that
 should go away. She
 didn't feel that

00:33:31.670 --> 00:33:35.959
everything should stay on
 the planet forever. And
 she thought that

00:33:35.960 --> 00:33:40.869
designers had a role
 in making data disappear. I
 wish more people had

00:33:40.870 --> 00:33:45.719
paid attention to that.
 I think that's really important.
 And in this book,

00:33:45.720 --> 00:33:50.359
which Aron will talk about
 more, we can kind of
 see the battle for

00:33:50.360 --> 00:33:56.669
authorship between two
 great women of design
 theory and thinking.

00:33:56.670 --> 00:34:03.169
Muriel Cooper and Denise
 Scott Brown, the real
 author and the graphic

00:34:03.170 --> 00:34:10.299
design author. And Muriel
 I think was frustrated by
 being told what to

00:34:10.300 --> 00:34:16.209
do by the author.
 And she created this incredible
 book that provided a

00:34:16.210 --> 00:34:22.109
kind of new interface
 for connecting image and text,
 for allowing us to

00:34:22.110 --> 00:34:30.119
absorb the image as a
 kind of text. And to
 kind of fit those things

00:34:30.120 --> 00:34:34.219
together in an incredibly eloquent
 way So if you come
 in close to this

00:34:34.220 --> 00:34:39.399
book, you'll see that
 that wide letting made space
 for these little figure

00:34:39.400 --> 00:34:44.359
numbers which are keyed
 to the pictures. And it
 allowed the reader to

00:34:44.360 --> 00:34:48.929
go back and forth
 between the text and the
 image in an absolutely

00:34:48.930 --> 00:34:53.809
brilliant way. Apparently Denise
 Scott Brown didn't like
 that very much.

00:34:53.810 --> 00:35:00.049
And there exists in
 the papers of Muriel
 Cooper interchanges between

00:35:00.050 --> 00:35:05.459
these two great women
 fighting about the book.
 And Denise thought it

00:35:05.460 --> 00:35:11.559
was too interesting, too
 Bauhaus, too much noise
 of the graphic

00:35:11.560 --> 00:35:15.999
designer in there.
 And what she wanted
 was something more

00:35:16.000 --> 00:35:21.309
conventional. Which is what
 she got later. Right? I'll
 let Muriel have the

00:35:21.310 --> 00:35:28.189
last word on that. Talking
 about the author. And you
 can see here the

00:35:28.190 --> 00:35:34.039
kind of frustration of
 graphic designers feeling boxed
 in and told what

00:35:34.040 --> 00:35:38.479
to do. And what she
 really wanted is for us to
 all have tools to be

00:35:38.480 --> 00:35:45.399
authors and designers and
 publishers. And boy, did we
 get those tools. Okay?

00:35:45.400 --> 00:35:47.659
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:35:47.660 --> 00:35:55.939
&gt;&gt; Thank you.

00:35:55.940 --> 00:35:58.409
&gt;&gt; Aron?
(Applause).

00:35:58.410 --> 00:36:00.799
&gt;&gt; Thank you
 very much. I came to
 Muriel Cooper's work directly

00:36:00.800 --> 00:36:04.399
through her design work.
 I never met her
 in person. I first encountered

00:36:04.400 --> 00:36:08.509
her actually when
 I started working on
 "Learning from Las Vegas."

00:36:08.510 --> 00:36:11.749
Like many people,
 I first encountered the
 book on the right,

00:36:11.750 --> 00:36:15.179
which is the
 revised version, the boring
 version I guess,

00:36:15.180 --> 00:36:21.239
that was designed
 by Denise Scott Brown
 And then worked myself

00:36:21.240 --> 00:36:26.059
back to
 the original version designed
 by Muriel Cooper.

00:36:26.060 --> 00:36:29.909
And I guess I
 was kind of stunned by
 her approach to design

00:36:29.910 --> 00:36:36.709
and vision, which I
 initially, you know, was
 directed towards the design

00:36:36.710 --> 00:36:42.309
work. This kind of
 complicated I think prefiguring
 her later interests in

00:36:42.310 --> 00:36:46.669
the Visible Language
 Workshop on texture,
 layering, transparency, a

00:36:46.670 --> 00:36:54.169
kind of dynamism to
 the text, interrelations between
 text and image. As

00:36:54.170 --> 00:36:59.709
Ellen said, you can
 see the extreme letting, the
 triple spacing of the

00:36:59.710 --> 00:37:07.239
main text juxtaposed by the
 very tight text in the
 margins and so forth

00:37:07.240 --> 00:37:14.439
Here is another example
 of the layout of
 Muriel Cooper's design. And

00:37:14.440 --> 00:37:20.089
then you can see
 the difference that Denise Scott
 Brown really did not

00:37:20.090 --> 00:37:24.689
like the Swiss treatment
 of her book. And really
 wanted a much more

00:37:24.690 --> 00:37:29.009
boring or she called
 it deadpan approach to
 the book with very

00:37:29.010 --> 00:37:37.809
regularized text and image.
 The kind of --
 the images grouped together

00:37:37.810 --> 00:37:47.059
here. Here you can see
 the use of the grid. This
 was drawn for me by

00:37:47.060 --> 00:37:50.819
Sally Steiner in showing
 you the grid that Muriel
 Cooper used. And then

00:37:50.820 --> 00:37:56.399
again, this image, which
 was also supposed to
 kind of replicate the

00:37:56.400 --> 00:38:00.169
movement through the city and
 the lines of the text
 are kind of the

00:38:00.170 --> 00:38:04.069
roadways or the highways.
 And the numbering of
 the images are meant

00:38:04.070 --> 00:38:08.569
to be like signs. So
 your way of reading is
 like moving through the city.

00:38:08.570 --> 00:38:13.719
And this kind of
 sense of movement is also
 kind of demonstrated in

00:38:13.720 --> 00:38:18.969
these layouts. This is a
 layout for Part 1 of
 "Learning from Las Vegas."

00:38:18.970 --> 00:38:23.429
So Muriel Cooper could
 see the arrangements of
 text and image and

00:38:23.430 --> 00:38:28.079
flow and so forth.
 And the filmic dimension is
 really important here. But

00:38:28.080 --> 00:38:32.529
I think I want to
 mention what I learned from her,
 what I think about her

00:38:32.530 --> 00:38:36.399
work that's important. And
 this is the kind
 of triadic relationship

00:38:36.400 --> 00:38:41.629
between communication, movement
 and experience. And I
 think I have

00:38:41.630 --> 00:38:45.499
a little bit of
 a different attitude to what
 communication is perhaps than

00:38:45.500 --> 00:38:48.659
traditional designers. I'm not
 a designer. And to
 me communication is

00:38:48.660 --> 00:38:52.779
exposing us to
 the risk of experience.
 That means traversing

00:38:52.780 --> 00:38:58.529
something, pushing against
 limits, undergoing something.
 And this

00:38:58.530 --> 00:39:04.419
kind of attitude towards
 experience really is about
 discovering a path.

00:39:04.420 --> 00:39:10.599
It's not formulating a
 method. It is not
 deploying kind of preconceived

00:39:10.600 --> 00:39:15.239
formats, whether those are grids
 or not. It is really
 the exposure for us

00:39:15.240 --> 00:39:20.019
to experience something new.
 And to undergo it.
 Not to have an

00:39:20.020 --> 00:39:25.569
experience. But to -- and
 not to own it or
 anything like that. But to

00:39:25.570 --> 00:39:32.119
actually undergo it and
 move along a path. So
 a hodos (phonetic), a

00:39:32.120 --> 00:39:36.409
path. Method -- the
 word method consists of the
 word hodos, which is

00:39:36.410 --> 00:39:40.819
path And I
 don't think she ever
 deployed anything preconceived

00:39:40.820 --> 00:39:46.179
Although of course she
 was deploying Swiss kind
 of graphic design.

00:39:46.180 --> 00:39:50.029
But the kind of material
 kind of spills out over
 there. And I think what

00:39:50.030 --> 00:39:55.039
she brought to her
 further work, Visible Language
 Workshop, is that the

00:39:55.040 --> 00:39:59.599
book was really undergoing
 a rapid change in
 1972. Things were

00:39:59.600 --> 00:40:04.439
opening up. And this was
 a site of experiment. The
 book was no longer

00:40:04.440 --> 00:40:10.539
an archaic stillness. But
 was opening up and kind
 of proliferating by the

00:40:10.540 --> 00:40:17.879
work of people like,
 you know, designers at the
 time and theorists and

00:40:17.880 --> 00:40:22.349
so forth. And Muriel Cooper
 was at the forefront of
 that. And that kind

00:40:22.350 --> 00:40:28.609
of issue about movement
 and information and complexity,
 a book isn't

00:40:28.610 --> 00:40:32.999
just to be read
 for information. But something like
 the size of the

00:40:33.000 --> 00:40:37.219
"Learning from Las Vegas"
 book, the original version,
 you can't sit there

00:40:37.220 --> 00:40:41.899
and read it. You need to
 place it on a table You
 need to move it around.

00:40:41.900 --> 00:40:46.099
Explore it. Move through
 the information. And this
 kind of attitude gets

00:40:46.100 --> 00:40:49.769
carried on and carried
 forth in her later work
 with the Visible Language

00:40:49.770 --> 00:40:55.449
Workshop But a lot
 of that was enabled by
 her exploration of book

00:40:55.450 --> 00:41:01.839
design I think. So that's
 what I was impressed with
 with her work and

00:41:01.840 --> 00:41:04.879
my encounter
 with her
 work.

00:41:04.880 --> 00:41:07.469
&gt;&gt; Thanks, Aron. Ben?

00:41:07.470 --> 00:41:11.809
 (Applause).

00:41:11.810 --> 00:41:15.909
&gt;&gt; So I
 don't typically read when
 doing a talk.

00:41:15.910 --> 00:41:20.069
I've been ill lately
 and have a four-month-old
 at home so

00:41:20.070 --> 00:41:23.609
I have the memory of
 a mosquito. And I want
 to make sure that

00:41:23.610 --> 00:41:27.539
I give this subject its
 due here. So at the
 risk of dating myself,

00:41:27.540 --> 00:41:35.649
I think I probably saw the
 work of Muriel and the VLW
 first in a Wired magazine.

00:41:35.650 --> 00:41:41.049
A couple of years
 later, I was a
 senior at Carnegie-Mellon

00:41:41.050 --> 00:41:45.649
and recently VLW
 alum. Suguru Ishizaki
 brought David Small

00:41:45.650 --> 00:41:49.089
and John Maeda to speak.
 And this was the first
 time that I saw

00:41:49.090 --> 00:41:52.559
how my interest
 in graphic design and
 Computer Science might

00:41:52.560 --> 00:41:56.859
actually be combined in
 a more interesting way
 than sort of jamming

00:41:56.860 --> 00:42:01.429
them together. As a
 student I had already
 worked professionally as a

00:42:01.430 --> 00:42:05.659
graphic designer and
 separately as a software
 developer. But always

00:42:05.660 --> 00:42:08.019
kind of hated computer
 graphics. Because I found
 them to represent

00:42:08.020 --> 00:42:10.719
everything I despised about
 the usual collision of
 art and engineering.

00:42:10.720 --> 00:42:13.799
A bunch of engineers
 trying to replicate a
 Mondriaan painting and

00:42:13.800 --> 00:42:17.689
calling it art or
 3D graphics with spears
 and cubes and incandescent

00:42:17.690 --> 00:42:21.999
RGB colors So synthetic
 and alien that they hardly
 seem at home with

00:42:22.000 --> 00:42:25.919
the computer itself. I
 loved how Muriel remained a
 designer first. And it

00:42:25.920 --> 00:42:29.159
shows the influence of
 her work as seen
 in students. Often when

00:42:29.160 --> 00:42:32.519
designers start working
 with computation as a
 medium, there's a

00:42:32.520 --> 00:42:35.559
tendency to shut off
 their design intention because
 they are in this f

00:42:35.560 --> 00:42:39.799
oreign and sort of
 technical space. To make
 the transition, you really

00:42:39.800 --> 00:42:43.049
need to think --
 keep your critical eye and
 treat computation as just

00:42:43.050 --> 00:42:46.819
another medium that is
 as imperfect as any other.
 And learn from its

00:42:46.820 --> 00:42:50.849
working within its constraints
 by also trying to
 challenge yourself to

00:42:50.850 --> 00:42:54.699
overcome them. Muriel did
 this with her students
 with the VLW in

00:42:54.700 --> 00:42:57.589
exploring things like depth
 of field and blur in
 text. Many years before

00:42:57.590 --> 00:43:02.039
these would become
 commonplace. These came
 from an approach

00:43:02.040 --> 00:43:05.549
grounded in fields like
 photography and film and
 typography and a

00:43:05.550 --> 00:43:11.689
desire to make this cold
 medium a little bit more
 humanistic In 2001 I

00:43:11.690 --> 00:43:14.879
had been a student
 of John Maeda for three
 years and began working

00:43:14.880 --> 00:43:18.689
with Casey Reas on
 what would become Processing.
 A project that

00:43:18.690 --> 00:43:21.499
seeks to make it
 easier to merge these disciplines
 of design and code.

00:43:21.500 --> 00:43:24.969
And it is a direct
 descendant of coding libraries that
 date to the VLW.

00:43:24.970 --> 00:43:28.789
Plus the pedagogical side of
 what we did at the
 ACG. And finally, the

00:43:28.790 --> 00:43:31.709
desire to sketch with
 code. So writing short
 programs that are easily

00:43:31.710 --> 00:43:38.019
iterated upon, the same
 way that you work in
 your sketch book. The

00:43:38.020 --> 00:43:42.369
VLW name even lives
 on as the font format
 used by Processing, which

00:43:42.370 --> 00:43:46.939
stores information in exactly
 the same way as the
 type used in that

00:43:46.940 --> 00:43:51.709
information landscape studio we
 saw earlier. Millions of
 users later, we

00:43:51.710 --> 00:43:55.779
have a Mission
 Statement for the Processing
 project which reads,

00:43:55.780 --> 00:43:59.039
Processing seeks to ruin
 the careers of talented
 designers by tempting

00:43:59.040 --> 00:44:01.669
them away from their
 usual tools and into the
 world of program and

00:44:01.670 --> 00:44:04.729
computation. Similarly, the
 project is designed to
 turn engineers and

00:44:04.730 --> 00:44:09.189
computer scientists to
 less gainful employment
 as artists and designers.

00:44:09.190 --> 00:44:11.279
 (Chuckles).

00:44:11.280 --> 00:44:13.849
&gt;&gt; And I
 hope that point would resonate
 with Muriel. After she took

00:44:13.850 --> 00:44:16.019
a class with
 Nicholas Negroponte, she's quoted
 to have said the

00:44:16.020 --> 00:44:19.349
code on
 screen didn't make
 any Goddamn sense.

00:44:19.350 --> 00:44:21.369
 (Chuckles).

00:44:21.370 --> 00:44:24.259
&gt;&gt; I've always felt that these
 are precisely the people that I
 want to bring into the field.

00:44:24.260 --> 00:44:26.879
The technically inclined
 will always find
 their way regardless.

00:44:26.880 --> 00:44:30.069
But they will
 also make things that
 are more suited to their

00:44:30.070 --> 00:44:33.529
technical interests. So
 a field gets interesting,
 however, and only really

00:44:33.530 --> 00:44:38.679
truly evolves when it
 expands by bringing in
 people with different abilities

00:44:38.680 --> 00:44:42.209
and kinds of
 experiences. And as far
 as accessibility, we have

00:44:42.210 --> 00:44:46.049
come a long way
 since the VLW era where
 this work was limited to

00:44:46.050 --> 00:44:50.409
research labs using
 computers the size of a
 mini fridge. By the early

00:44:50.410 --> 00:44:53.469
2000s you could buy
 a computer for a few
 hundred dollars to do this

00:44:53.470 --> 00:44:56.279
type of work, which
 means that the means was
 in place. We just provided

00:44:56.280 --> 00:45:00.259
the platform -- or
 we just needed to
 provide the platform,

00:45:00.260 --> 00:45:03.759
which Casey and I
 sought to do with Processing.
 And now in the

00:45:03.760 --> 00:45:08.039
current decade the
 means is ubiquitous and
 Processing continues with

00:45:08.040 --> 00:45:11.759
initiatives like the p5.js
 project which takes the same
 ideas and tries to

00:45:11.760 --> 00:45:16.619
rethink them for
 sort of contemporary web
 setting. But more

00:45:16.620 --> 00:45:19.859
importantly, Lauren McCarthy
 has led the project
 with renewed focus

00:45:19.860 --> 00:45:23.069
on inclusion and diversity.
 Meaning that we're at a
 point where we have

00:45:23.070 --> 00:45:26.259
more room to consider
 what this means socially and
 how we can really

00:45:26.260 --> 00:45:29.879
extend the boundary of
 those participating in the
 field. The evolution

00:45:29.880 --> 00:45:32.819
over the last couple
 of decades reminds me
 of a wonderful John

00:45:32.820 --> 00:45:36.139
Adams quote, the paraphrase
 goes something like, I
 must study politics

00:45:36.140 --> 00:45:39.149
so that sons have
 something like liberty to
 study mathematics and

00:45:39.150 --> 00:45:42.319
philosophy. And my
 sons will study geography
 and commerce and

00:45:42.320 --> 00:45:46.389
agriculture to give their
 children the right to
 study painting, poetry,

00:45:46.390 --> 00:45:51.629
music and architecture. We
 owe an enormous debt
 to Muriel and her

00:45:51.630 --> 00:45:55.299
students for providing this
 basis to us and
 I'm honored to be

00:45:55.300 --> 00:45:57.939
participating today Thank you.

00:45:57.940 --> 00:46:05.449
 (Applause).

00:46:05.450 --> 00:46:10.129
&gt;&gt; When Ellen
 and then Aron were
 talking about the tension

00:46:10.130 --> 00:46:13.929
that occurred
 between Muriel Cooper
 and Denise Scott Brown

00:46:13.930 --> 00:46:17.639
and the
 design and production of
 "Learning from Las Vegas,"

00:46:17.640 --> 00:46:21.459
one of the things
 that actually throws in a
 sharp relief is that period

00:46:21.460 --> 00:46:28.859
when authors actually
 did turn over to
 designers a pile of

00:46:28.860 --> 00:46:32.269
manuscripts that
 looked like that photograph
 that Ellen showed.

00:46:32.270 --> 00:46:35.599
And then there was
 no choice but to have
 a designer or some appointed

00:46:35.600 --> 00:46:40.229
professional with
 Archaean technical skills,
 knowing the names

00:46:40.230 --> 00:46:45.269
of typefaces, knowing
 how to do color
 separations and how

00:46:45.270 --> 00:46:48.449
to create mechanical
 artwork that could communicate
 intention to a

00:46:48.450 --> 00:46:52.399
printer. All of those things
 were like what Abby Miller
 calls black arts in

00:46:52.400 --> 00:46:58.769
a way. That civilians
 didn't know and weren't
 meant to know. And

00:46:58.770 --> 00:47:02.649
certainly authors weren't meant
 to know or even
 have an opinion about.

00:47:02.650 --> 00:47:06.159
So hence, that collision was
 so acute I think. Now
 -- and then as Ben

00:47:06.160 --> 00:47:13.059
just said, now we're
 in a period where indeed
 the means of production

00:47:13.060 --> 00:47:19.499
are in everyone's hands. And
 is it for the better
 do you think, Ben?

00:47:19.500 --> 00:47:27.329
&gt;&gt; No question. Because
 I think the --
 again, it's --

00:47:27.330 --> 00:47:30.979
it only gets interesting
 as you bring the other
 kinds of people into it.

00:47:30.980 --> 00:47:35.199
So it's kind
 of a -- you know,
 you have people who come

00:47:35.200 --> 00:47:38.569
in who are
 beginners, but they are
 experts somewhere else.

00:47:38.570 --> 00:47:41.899
So I think it's
 really fascinating to take somebody
 who has that --

00:47:41.900 --> 00:47:48.909
something that they are expert
 at but they are kind
 of bringing that into something new

00:47:48.910 --> 00:47:54.189
And that's only
 -- you know, so the
 idea that the --

00:47:54.190 --> 00:47:58.599
where Muriel was bringing
 things like from typography,
 just obvious things,

00:47:58.600 --> 00:48:02.119
for somebody who
 has worked with
 film or photography,

00:48:02.120 --> 00:48:07.669
that motion blur and
 depth of field are just,
 you know, the norm.

00:48:07.670 --> 00:48:12.059
But for somebody
 who is thinking about
 things from an engineering

00:48:12.060 --> 00:48:15.819
perspective, well, I'm
 trying to get the shapes
 on screen And it's like,

00:48:15.820 --> 00:48:19.809
well -- so
 being able to kind of
 merge those things together

00:48:19.810 --> 00:48:23.469
in a way that it's
 just that's what you get
 at that sort of intersection

00:48:23.470 --> 00:48:28.509
essentially. So I think the
 only way you can do
 that is get as many

00:48:28.510 --> 00:48:31.879
people as possible kind
 of using these kinds
 of tools and making

00:48:31.880 --> 00:48:34.669
messes
 with them
 first.

00:48:34.670 --> 00:48:36.099
&gt;&gt; Aron?

00:48:36.100 --> 00:48:39.029
&gt;&gt; Just a clarification, the
 -- this is not an
 answer to that question.

00:48:39.030 --> 00:48:43.319
But a bit of
 detail with the "Learning
 from Las Vegas"

00:48:43.320 --> 00:48:47.669
book, it definitely wasn't
 dumping a pile of papers
 like these are people

00:48:47.670 --> 00:48:52.779
who had very
 strong Denise Scott Brown,
 Robert Ventura, had very

00:48:52.780 --> 00:48:56.039
strong design
 ideas and thought of
 themselves as designers.

00:48:56.040 --> 00:48:58.159
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:48:58.160 --> 00:49:01.479
&gt;&gt; So it was very
 overdeveloped or thought about in
 kind of very specific

00:49:01.480 --> 00:49:05.819
ways by
 the time it
 arrived there.

00:49:05.820 --> 00:49:09.819
&gt;&gt; That's interesting. And
 this takes us down
 kind of a dangerously

00:49:09.820 --> 00:49:12.369
Archaean
 graphic
 design road.

00:49:12.370 --> 00:49:14.349
&gt;&gt; Love it; love it.

00:49:14.350 --> 00:49:19.429
&gt;&gt; I figured that,
 Ellen. Good. But I think
 that it's easy if you're

00:49:19.430 --> 00:49:23.069
a graphic designer to
 look at Muriel's design
 for that book as being

00:49:23.070 --> 00:49:28.729
the designed one.
 And to look at the
 second -- the revised

00:49:28.730 --> 00:49:32.659
edition and the one that
 I, in fact, first saw
 and only knew for many

00:49:32.660 --> 00:49:37.429
years as being sort of
 like this undesigned piece of
 crap that was like

00:49:37.430 --> 00:49:41.209
done -- put together
 by untutored people. But when
 -- you know, the

00:49:41.210 --> 00:49:48.639
intention behind it was very,
 very powerful in terms --
 and Denise Scott Brown's

00:49:48.640 --> 00:49:52.639
objections to Muriel's design
 were really articulated and
 very -- can you

00:49:52.640 --> 00:49:57.729
sort of describe
 what Denise wanted out
 of that book?

00:49:57.730 --> 00:50:01.069
&gt;&gt; I think it's all a
 matter of timing.

00:50:01.070 --> 00:50:04.479
So actually it's odd
 because much is made
 of the conflict between

00:50:04.480 --> 00:50:08.639
Muriel Cooper and
 Denise Scott Brown
 as Muriel Cooper said,

00:50:08.640 --> 00:50:14.299
it was a battle of
 wits, you know. And it was
 a really kind of --

00:50:14.300 --> 00:50:17.699
a real conflict. But
 you know, they had
 many things in common.

00:50:17.700 --> 00:50:23.669
I could go
 through them. It's interest in
 movement. They both were

00:50:23.670 --> 00:50:28.079
interested in multiple
 graphic modes of
 representing or

00:50:28.080 --> 00:50:34.969
accounting for sensory
 plenitude and information
 landscapes. I could

00:50:34.970 --> 00:50:37.999
go on and on. So why
 was the conflict? And of course
 a lot of it is

00:50:38.000 --> 00:50:42.979
about timing and so forth.
 You know, that second book
 you could say is

00:50:42.980 --> 00:50:48.329
a treatise. It's an idea of
 a text. It wants to be
 a book. You know, it

00:50:48.330 --> 00:50:54.099
wants to get
 across a set of
 premises or polemics.

00:50:54.100 --> 00:50:57.649
&gt;&gt; It's also
 more accessible. It's
 a little paperback.

00:50:57.650 --> 00:50:59.919
 &gt;&gt; Inexpensive.

00:50:59.920 --> 00:51:03.539
&gt;&gt; In keeping away
 from the more pragmatic economics
 and things like that.

00:51:03.540 --> 00:51:06.769
&gt;&gt; But that's key I think
 as a reader. As much as
 I love the big book,

00:51:06.770 --> 00:51:10.709
I don't want
 to actually interact with
 it that much.

00:51:10.710 --> 00:51:13.519
&gt;&gt; But the other
 one was a kind
 of sense of the text.

00:51:13.520 --> 00:51:18.149
Or what JJ
 Gibson would call a sensory
 ecology that you don't get

00:51:18.150 --> 00:51:23.429
text purified of its
 sensory plenitude. And that's what
 you get in the

00:51:23.430 --> 00:51:31.129
first book is a kind
 of sense of the text.
 And the second book,

00:51:31.130 --> 00:51:36.469
you get the idea of a
 text or a treatise or a
 polemic. And those are kind

00:51:36.470 --> 00:51:42.279
of different desires but
 originally Denise Scott Brown
 they wanted a

00:51:42.280 --> 00:51:45.109
huge book
 right at
 the beginning.

00:51:45.110 --> 00:51:47.069
&gt;&gt; They got it.

00:51:47.070 --> 00:51:49.479
 (Chuckles).

00:51:49.480 --> 00:51:52.399
&gt;&gt; Yeah, belatedly. And
 so these kinds of sense
 of timings and what

00:51:52.400 --> 00:51:58.769
you want to kind
 of achieve polemically with your
 body of work shifts overtime

00:51:58.770 --> 00:52:02.819
&gt;&gt; What's
 interesting is the
 revised edition, there's nothing

00:52:02.820 --> 00:52:06.799
to do
 with that book
 except read it. Right?

00:52:06.800 --> 00:52:10.889
Like looking at it
 is no fun. For me
 at least. There's some plates

00:52:10.890 --> 00:52:13.779
in it that you
 can look at. But basically
 you have no choice --

00:52:13.780 --> 00:52:16.479
if you want to
 engage with that book, you
 have one way of doing it,

00:52:16.480 --> 00:52:19.109
which is reading
 it. Whereas I would
 maintain in the First

00:52:19.110 --> 00:52:22.119
Edition and newly rereleased
 is you can really
 engage with that book

00:52:22.120 --> 00:52:24.919
without actually reading it.
 And without actually perceiving
 any of the

00:52:24.920 --> 00:52:28.179
ideas, in fact. And people
 will -- you know, have
 done and will do that.

00:52:28.180 --> 00:52:35.239
So I sort of get
 -- those two ways of
 looking at text is really

00:52:35.240 --> 00:52:39.829
pronounced. And I think one
 of the things that I
 wonder is was -- and

00:52:39.830 --> 00:52:48.769
Ben, I'll put this to
 you. Was she a reader? Was
 she frustrated by kind of

00:52:48.770 --> 00:52:55.599
the linear nature of
 a traditional book? And
 that information landscape

00:52:55.600 --> 00:52:59.909
&gt;&gt; I didn't
 get the opportunity to
 work with her

00:52:59.910 --> 00:53:01.929
&gt;&gt; But do
 you get a sense
 from the --

00:53:01.930 --> 00:53:04.599
&gt;&gt; I think from the --
I think what's interesting
about the information

00:53:04.600 --> 00:53:09.529
landscape and -- Richard
 touched on it earlier as
 far as this idea of

00:53:09.530 --> 00:53:13.239
how you actually move
 through a space of information.
 And I think it's

00:53:13.240 --> 00:53:17.449
kind of -- for people
 who like working with information,
 that that's kind of --

00:53:17.450 --> 00:53:20.829
these are the things that
 roll around in their heads
 -- our heads --

00:53:20.830 --> 00:53:25.429
and keep us up at
 night. So I think that
 was the kind of thing

00:53:25.430 --> 00:53:29.069
that's kind of rolling around for
 her. And that this is like
 -- this is just the

00:53:29.070 --> 00:53:32.709
way it's working in her
 head Then she makes a layout
 for a book like the

00:53:32.710 --> 00:53:39.279
Las Vegas book, that's kind
 of a moment frozen in
 time of that picture.

00:53:39.280 --> 00:53:45.139
It's been in constant motion.
 And then all of a
 sudden here is this

00:53:45.140 --> 00:53:48.379
snapshot that we're putting out
 in front of people. So
 I think that sort of

00:53:48.380 --> 00:53:52.889
thing, you know, is
 really what comes through. How
 do you capture that

00:53:52.890 --> 00:53:56.769
motion and
 movement through
 the information?

00:53:56.770 --> 00:54:02.299
&gt;&gt; I think Nicholas
 and Ricky both kind of
 referred to the revelatory quality

00:54:02.300 --> 00:54:07.719
of that presentation
 of information landscapes the
 first time at TED5.

00:54:07.720 --> 00:54:12.879
And what strikes me
 if you sort of see
 it today, it's --

00:54:12.880 --> 00:54:20.819
there's not a single
 kind of, you know,
 candy bar commercial on

00:54:20.820 --> 00:54:24.589
television that doesn't use
 some sort of --
 kind of move that kind

00:54:24.590 --> 00:54:30.479
of first saw the light
 of day in that thing. So
 have we reached some sort

00:54:30.480 --> 00:54:40.419
of endpoint? Ellen, as
 a curative member, have
 we reached some

00:54:40.420 --> 00:54:44.149
sort of endpoint where
 now we await the
 next thing, the next kind

00:54:44.150 --> 00:54:47.609
of transformation?
 Which may be
 just emojis or something?

00:54:47.610 --> 00:54:50.629
 I don't know.

00:54:50.630 --> 00:54:52.519
 (Chuckles).

00:54:52.520 --> 00:54:56.059
&gt;&gt; Well I think
 now we're talking about
 now having text everywhere

00:54:56.060 --> 00:54:58.519
on our glasses and
 we're going to look
 around and everything

00:54:58.520 --> 00:55:02.159
is going to have text
 all over it. I think
 I'm waiting for that.

00:55:02.160 --> 00:55:04.039
&gt;&gt; With enthusiasm?

00:55:04.040 --> 00:55:05.659
&gt;&gt; Sure; sure.

00:55:05.660 --> 00:55:07.599
 (Chuckles)

00:55:07.600 --> 00:55:09.719
&gt;&gt; Yeah,
 I think there's
 always this hunger fo

00:55:09.720 --> 00:55:14.149
r more and different.
 But I do think that
 that paradigm of the information

00:55:14.150 --> 00:55:19.819
landscape from that
 conference, what was it,
 30 years ago,

00:55:19.820 --> 00:55:24.799
was like a
 profound moment. Because
 it wasn't just layers

00:55:24.800 --> 00:55:30.479
of text that were
 parallel to the screen. But
 the fact of the moving

00:55:30.480 --> 00:55:38.949
like this was mind
 blowing. Because it was
 just taking the perspective

00:55:38.950 --> 00:55:45.089
in a different way.
 And that it was live text.
 It wasn't pictures of text.

00:55:45.090 --> 00:55:49.689
It was actual
 language that could
 be edited.

00:55:49.690 --> 00:55:53.559
&gt;&gt; I think, also,
 a lot of what
 you see in the advertising

00:55:53.560 --> 00:55:57.269
and sort of
 the versions of this that
 are really pervasive are

00:55:57.270 --> 00:56:00.589
really kind of the
 side effect of it.
 You love when type moves

00:56:00.590 --> 00:56:03.709
around in 3D
 on a screen We're
 very drawn to it.

00:56:03.710 --> 00:56:08.259
And we're really drawn
 to language and things like
 that. But I think the

00:56:08.260 --> 00:56:11.789
fundamental kinds of
 issues that Muriel and
 VLW were going after

00:56:11.790 --> 00:56:15.059
as far as
 how do you actually move
 through a space of information?

00:56:15.060 --> 00:56:19.299
How do you
 actually interact with it?
 How do you

00:56:19.300 --> 00:56:22.349
understand this? We're still
 a long ways off from
 actually fixing a lot

00:56:22.350 --> 00:56:26.699
more of those core ideas.
 And how do we spend
 more time kind of

00:56:26.700 --> 00:56:30.439
focused on unpacking some
 of those issues that they
 had just kind of

00:56:30.440 --> 00:56:33.839
started to
 scratch the
 surface of?

00:56:33.840 --> 00:56:39.039
&gt;&gt; I think
 sometimes when the argument
 of how she progressed

00:56:39.040 --> 00:56:41.929
from book design
 with the VLW, that
 it was a limit.

00:56:41.930 --> 00:56:45.569
The book was too still.
 All kinds of things that
 stayed -- were kind of

00:56:45.570 --> 00:56:52.229
intervening on the
 kinds of movement
 that she wanted.

00:56:52.230 --> 00:56:58.129
But I actually think
 actually some of that
 stillness remained in the

00:56:58.130 --> 00:57:02.959
design. And I think that's
 what's been lost. This kind
 of gap or temporal

00:57:02.960 --> 00:57:07.849
gap between the acquisition
 of data and the
 plane of discovery. And

00:57:07.850 --> 00:57:12.109
those have been compressed.
 And here with her
 work, you always had

00:57:12.110 --> 00:57:17.379
that -- the time to
 do that. And that temporal
 kind of extension and

00:57:17.380 --> 00:57:23.109
duration has been rapidly
 closing. And I think that
 that's an issue or

00:57:23.110 --> 00:57:28.379
what gives her work
 that kind of interest
 or depth I think

00:57:28.380 --> 00:57:31.429
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I
 think when I look
 at her --

00:57:31.430 --> 00:57:37.189
at Muriel Cooper's later
 work when it's the screen-based
 work and the digital work,

00:57:37.190 --> 00:57:41.679
it always really seems
 to have like if not
 a literary root to it,

00:57:41.680 --> 00:57:45.229
at least it's sort
 of thought -- you understand
 that the information is

00:57:45.230 --> 00:57:50.389
meant to be understood and
 interpreted. And I think if
 you sort of want

00:57:50.390 --> 00:57:53.969
to take a writeup of
 the presenter day, you know,
 one of the reasons that

00:57:53.970 --> 00:57:59.339
we have this crisis of
 what is truth and what
 is fake news is that information

00:57:59.340 --> 00:58:04.259
comes to people
 from all sorts of different
 angles, links to things

00:58:04.260 --> 00:58:06.539
&gt;&gt; It always looks good.

00:58:06.540 --> 00:58:08.729
&gt;&gt; Yeah. And
 things are linked
 off of things.

00:58:08.730 --> 00:58:11.759
And they all -- we
 can admit, they are all sort
 of coming out of the

00:58:11.760 --> 00:58:13.939
same pipe. And people
 make up their own
 minds about what's true

00:58:13.940 --> 00:58:18.169
or not. And I think
 that's sort of what is the
 authorial -- the authority that

00:58:18.170 --> 00:58:25.189
authors bring to things.
 No mistake those words
 I think have the

00:58:25.190 --> 00:58:28.819
same root Which
 the difference between
 the treatise and

00:58:28.820 --> 00:58:34.929
a pile of manuscript I think
 is sort of a little bit
 about -- you know, that

00:58:34.930 --> 00:58:40.079
gap defines the
 confusion that pervades
 the world today.

00:58:40.080 --> 00:58:43.189
&gt;&gt; Good.

00:58:43.190 --> 00:58:46.779
&gt;&gt; Can I ask a question?

00:58:46.780 --> 00:58:48.519
&gt;&gt; Please.

00:58:48.520 --> 00:58:53.019
&gt;&gt; Go ahead, Ricky.

00:58:53.020 --> 00:58:54.889
 &gt;&gt; (Off microphone).

00:58:54.890 --> 00:58:56.719
&gt;&gt;
 Yeah.

00:58:56.720 --> 00:58:59.902
&gt;&gt; (Off microphone) and
that conversation was

00:58:59.903 --> 00:59:03.359
made (inaudible) of having
spoke to each other

00:59:03.360 --> 00:59:07.479
 (off microphone).

00:59:07.480 --> 00:59:09.899
 &gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:59:09.900 --> 00:59:12.029
&gt;&gt; It was a live event.

00:59:12.030 --> 00:59:14.461
&gt;&gt; Yeah, so Ricky points out,
if you didn't hear it, it was
really an attempt to create a

00:59:14.462 --> 00:59:16.919
conversation on the page, a
conversation with the text,
and sort of the ideal almost as

00:59:16.920 --> 00:59:20.839
Talmudic commentary on
 a set text.
 And I would guess

00:59:20.840 --> 00:59:23.709
there are some
 authors that actually don't
 like that, they don't

00:59:23.710 --> 00:59:27.069
want to have
 a conversation, they
 just want --

00:59:27.070 --> 00:59:28.709
&gt;&gt;
 Yeah, leave
 me alone.

00:59:28.710 --> 00:59:30.579
&gt;&gt; Yeah, leave me
 alone and let me just
 say my piece and read

00:59:30.580 --> 00:59:33.247
all the way through. And that wasn't
Muriel's point of view. Muriel's

00:59:33.248 --> 00:59:35.989
point of view, I think, was
to enable people to
access text in a lot

00:59:35.990 --> 00:59:39.159
of different
 ways and to interact
 with it actively.

00:59:39.160 --> 00:59:40.519
 &gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:59:40.520 --> 00:59:49.549
&gt;&gt; So is there one big lesson
that we can learn from Muriel
that would solve our problems

00:59:49.550 --> 00:59:53.899
as they
 are currently
 developed? Ellen?

00:59:53.900 --> 00:59:59.640
&gt;&gt; I think what was really
driving so much of her work
was the desire to break down the

01:00:00.000 --> 01:00:08.429
mediation between authorship and
 getting it out there.
 And the idea that

01:00:08.430 --> 01:00:16.789
you could have direct access
 to both the content and
 its delivery. I think

01:00:16.790 --> 01:00:21.499
that was just a driving
 thing. That she wanted to
 get rid of that person

01:00:21.500 --> 01:00:26.289
you have to call up
 and ask, oh, I have a
 typo, will you fix it?

01:00:26.290 --> 01:00:28.219
 &gt;&gt; Yeah.

01:00:28.220 --> 01:00:30.339
&gt;&gt; Right? People
 don't want to have
 to do that.

01:00:30.340 --> 01:00:32.329
They don't want to have
 to call Michael Bierut and
 say, fix my typo.

01:00:32.330 --> 01:00:36.749
They just want to be
 able to fix it. And
 I think that desire to

01:00:36.750 --> 01:00:42.139
get rid of
 that mediation was really
 profound for her.

01:00:42.140 --> 01:00:47.379
And it's really important
 to me as an
 author who also uses InDesign.

01:00:47.380 --> 01:00:50.569
I want to
 fix my own typos,
 thank you, Muriel

01:00:50.570 --> 01:00:54.509
&gt;&gt; Ben, Aron?

01:00:54.510 --> 01:00:58.549
&gt;&gt; So most
 of my work is about
 sort of understanding --

01:00:58.550 --> 01:01:04.239
working with
 information. And basically
 this idea

01:01:04.240 --> 01:01:08.029
of there's this mess
 of information out there.
 And that design is

01:01:08.030 --> 01:01:12.069
essentially the lens through
 which the -- the
 only lens through which we're

01:01:12.070 --> 01:01:15.769
going to get
 understanding out of it. As
 far as I'm concerned,

01:01:15.770 --> 01:01:20.079
Muriel really represents sort
 of a patron saint of
 that type of thinking

01:01:20.080 --> 01:01:25.079
And that way of
 how an individual actually
 interacts with information as

01:01:25.080 --> 01:01:33.649
meditated by code and
 by the computer as a
 digital tool. It's remarkable.

01:01:33.650 --> 01:01:38.849
&gt;&gt; Yeah, for me, I
 think it's that communication is
 not just conveying information.

01:01:38.850 --> 01:01:42.639
But as I said,
 exposing us to the
 risk of experience.

01:01:42.640 --> 01:01:46.449
And that it's --
 as you pointed out --
 a conversation, an encounter

01:01:46.450 --> 01:01:56.199
And those kinds of
 things are precious. And
 need to be fostered.

01:01:56.200 --> 01:02:04.549
And that illegibles
 and incommunicabilities are
 not external areas

01:02:04.550 --> 01:02:08.959
but they are
 generated internally. And those
 are productive of some

01:02:08.960 --> 01:02:12.559
of our most
 interesting encounters. So when
 those texts are

01:02:12.560 --> 01:02:16.189
shifting, there are
 certain points where polemicist
 tap-ins, where it

01:02:16.190 --> 01:02:20.249
erases information and just
 doesn't reveal it. And
 those are important

01:02:20.250 --> 01:02:26.159
points. And I think
 those opacities, blind spots,
 illegibles are kind of

01:02:26.160 --> 01:02:31.999
being wiped out and
 we need to find new
 ways of preserving those

01:02:32.000 --> 01:02:35.909
kinds of things, difficulties.

01:02:35.910 --> 01:02:40.539
&gt;&gt; I would like
 to thank our panel, Aron
 Vinegar, Ben Fry, Ellen Lupton.

01:02:40.540 --> 01:02:47.069
 Please join me.

01:02:47.070 --> 01:02:49.209
 (Applause).

01:02:49.210 --> 01:02:52.679
&gt;&gt; Thank you very much.

01:02:52.680 --> 01:02:54.699
&gt;&gt; That was good.

01:02:54.700 --> 01:02:57.259
&gt;&gt; Thank you.

