So I hung out in
 the lobby, just sitting there in
 the Wiesner sort of like

01:10:22.140 --> 01:10:27.669
special place Who knew
 Jerry Wiesner? Who knew
 this person? So an

01:10:27.670 --> 01:10:32.889
amazing figure of MIT
 Stood for world peace,
 like really important stuff,

01:10:32.890 --> 01:10:36.959
right? So I just hung
 out there and worked. And
 as I worked there, it's

01:10:36.960 --> 01:10:44.159
great because when you're in
 this little place, suddenly you
 see all of the

01:10:44.160 --> 01:10:48.239
details. Because I've been
 here like four or five
 times. But by sitting

01:10:48.240 --> 01:10:51.979
there, I also looked at
 a video conference and I'm
 like, wait, there's a

01:10:51.980 --> 01:10:55.769
picture. And I said this
 picture. And this is like
 a really good picture. This

01:10:55.770 --> 01:11:03.359
is like a good picture,
 right? And when I saw
 this picture, it reminded me

01:11:03.360 --> 01:11:09.699
of what, to me, MIT
 meant. Because I really had
 never heard of design

01:11:09.700 --> 01:11:15.699
when I came to
 MIT. I was a classical
 engineering student. My parents

01:11:15.700 --> 01:11:20.409
didn't have much money
 or education. So just
 coming here was the

01:11:20.410 --> 01:11:23.419
whole goal. By the time
 I got here, I wasn't sure
 what to do. But I

01:11:23.420 --> 01:11:28.059
remembered looking at the
 Media Lab and thinking,
 well, that's a really

01:11:28.060 --> 01:11:33.079
weird place. That
 seems unconventional -- very
 impractical. You know,

01:11:33.080 --> 01:11:37.119
like it's a big
 format. It's got images everywhere.
 I don't understand it.

01:11:37.120 --> 01:11:40.859
Let's make it cheaper and
 simpler. It's like I didn't
 know what that was.

01:11:40.860 --> 01:11:45.839
And I was talking to
 the person at the front.
 There was a person from

01:11:45.840 --> 01:11:50.179
member relations.
 And --
 Sharon.

01:11:50.180 --> 01:11:55.349
&gt;&gt; Who is
 she? What does
 she do?

01:11:55.350 --> 01:11:58.169
&gt;&gt; I just recently found out.

01:11:58.170 --> 01:11:59.999
&gt;&gt; What did you find out?

01:12:00.000 --> 01:12:01.639
&gt;&gt; Oh my God.

01:12:01.640 --> 01:12:03.349
&gt;&gt; She knows everything
 in the Media Lab. She's
 at the front desk.

01:12:03.350 --> 01:12:06.589
So I said, who
 is Muriel Cooper? And
 she wasn't sure.

01:12:06.590 --> 01:12:09.849
So we cut the
 camera then. And then
 we did Cut 2.

01:12:09.850 --> 01:12:14.899
&gt;&gt; Famous person.

01:12:14.900 --> 01:12:17.379
&gt;&gt; Famous person?

01:12:17.380 --> 01:12:19.329
&gt;&gt; With a wonderful legacy.

01:12:19.330 --> 01:12:22.769
&gt;&gt; How does someone
 have a wonderful legacy?
 What's the secret? &gt;

01:12:22.770 --> 01:12:30.919
&gt; Passion, enthusiasm, probably a
 little bit of arrogance. &gt;&gt;
 Hmmm . . .

01:12:30.920 --> 01:12:33.769
&gt;&gt; And persistence.

01:12:33.770 --> 01:12:37.169
&gt;&gt; What's your name?

01:12:37.170 --> 01:12:38.929
&gt;&gt; I'm Sharon.

01:12:38.930 --> 01:12:41.579
&gt;&gt; Applause for Sharon. (Applause).

01:12:41.580 --> 01:12:43.369
&gt;&gt; She just
 kind of nailed it. And
 I thought about these things.

01:12:43.370 --> 01:12:46.409
These four
 words. And I
 thought about passion.

01:12:46.410 --> 01:12:50.509
And to Nicholas' point,
 you know, I did
 have this very special professor

01:12:50.510 --> 01:12:57.789
here at the Media
 Lab who made my life a
 little bit difficult. In retrospect

01:12:57.790 --> 01:12:59.899
I'm very grateful,
 of course, everything is
 better in hindsight.

01:12:59.900 --> 01:13:04.329
But I decided to leave.
 But I didn't know what to
 do. And Muriel told me

01:13:04.330 --> 01:13:07.599
to go to art school.
 And it was the best
 advice to give to me.

01:13:07.600 --> 01:13:13.349
She said that I wouldn't
 learn what I was wanting
 to learn at a

01:13:13.350 --> 01:13:17.159
technical school. Because I
 had done all of
 the technical education. The

01:13:17.160 --> 01:13:21.179
problem with engineering education
 is you learn how
 to build anything.

01:13:21.180 --> 01:13:26.059
But you don't know what to
 build. Or why to build it.
 And so -- but in art

01:13:26.060 --> 01:13:30.089
school, it really changed my
 life really. So I thank
 Muriel. Now, this word,

01:13:30.090 --> 01:13:37.179
enthusiasm, is interesting.
 Because something about
 enthusiasm is

01:13:37.180 --> 01:13:41.789
something that you might
 have as a rising
 person, someone who is

01:13:41.790 --> 01:13:46.369
going to figure stuff
 out. And enthusiastic people
 tend to need

01:13:46.370 --> 01:13:51.879
aspirational people. Like how
 many people had a
 hero? Come on. Who

01:13:51.880 --> 01:13:55.739
had a hero? Heroes.
 So that hero was
 someone that was aspirational.

01:13:55.740 --> 01:13:59.449
And my friend told me
 recently, Jessie, my dear friend,
 an artist, told me

01:13:59.450 --> 01:14:03.659
how aspiration and
 inspiration are two different
 things. Aspiration is

01:14:03.660 --> 01:14:07.389
what you're trying to
 see and you understand.
 Inspiration is what

01:14:07.390 --> 01:14:18.229
awakens inside you and
 is the fire that someone
 else gave you through

01:14:18.230 --> 01:14:21.669
having that aspirational being
 So I remember like
 leaving here. Not sure

01:14:21.670 --> 01:14:26.489
what to do. In Japan.
 Rebuilt my career as a
 designer. I came back to t

01:14:26.490 --> 01:14:32.259
he computer. And figured out
 that the skill I had
 to make code, to Ben's

01:14:32.260 --> 01:14:37.739
point, I knew what to
 make suddenly. It was so
 much easier. And I

01:14:37.740 --> 01:14:42.069
began publishing things in
 Japan And I made this
 little book called "The

01:14:42.070 --> 01:14:46.719
Reactive Square," which is
 10 squares that respond
 to sound delivered

01:14:46.720 --> 01:14:51.139
on a floppy disk. I
 know, very ancient. But I
 remember like, this kind of

01:14:51.140 --> 01:14:56.269
feels right. You know?
 And I remember emailing Muriel
 and saying, hey, I

01:14:56.270 --> 01:15:02.219
think I figured it out.
 And she wrote back to me
 and said, show me. And

01:15:02.220 --> 01:15:08.149
then a month later, she
 passed away. So I remember
 when I heard there

01:15:08.150 --> 01:15:12.139
was this call for someone
 to somehow come here, I
 thought, I can never

01:15:12.140 --> 01:15:16.849
do that. That's impossible.
 But I would love to
 show my respect. And

01:15:16.850 --> 01:15:24.449
what happened is the day
 before I came to the
 Media Lab to give a

01:15:24.450 --> 01:15:30.039
faculty talk, I was visiting
 this idol that I had,
 Paul Rand. Again, I didn't

01:15:30.040 --> 01:15:34.509
know about design when I
 came to MIT. Once I
 saw the Media Lab's

01:15:34.510 --> 01:15:37.919
world, I thought, I want
 to learn a little bit
 more about design. And I

01:15:37.920 --> 01:15:41.699
found a book by Paul
 Rand by accident in the
 MIT's Rotch visual library. I

01:15:41.700 --> 01:15:45.749
don't know if it's still
 here. But I was like, whoa,
 what is this book? This

01:15:45.750 --> 01:15:49.419
book is so fascinating. Ah,
 design Really interesting. I think
 I want to do

01:15:49.420 --> 01:15:55.699
this. Well, anyways, years
 later in Japan the Paul
 Rand of Japan, Ikko

01:15:55.700 --> 01:16:00.799
Tanaka, befriended me. He
 said, hey, if you're going
 to the United States,

01:16:00.800 --> 01:16:05.939
you should visit my friend,
 Paul Rand. I said, cool. So
 I went to visit Mr.

01:16:05.940 --> 01:16:12.929
Rand. And the next day
 was my faculty talk. So what
 a good day to do

01:16:12.930 --> 01:16:18.289
like any job talk, right?
 I was like so happy. I
 like met my dream person.

01:16:18.290 --> 01:16:23.089
So when I came
 here, I wasn't nervous luckily.
 I should have been

01:16:23.090 --> 01:16:27.339
nervous. But anyways, that was
 a big moment. So when
 I arrived at MIT,

01:16:27.340 --> 01:16:34.049
I asked Nicholas if
 we could bring Paul Rand.
 And I remember Nicholas

01:16:34.050 --> 01:16:38.639
said, Paul Rand, is he
 still alive? Is he still around?
 I said, oh, yeah, he's

01:16:38.640 --> 01:16:43.509
around. And we were
 worried if people would come
 to see Paul Rand

01:16:43.510 --> 01:16:50.039
talk. And also Paul made
 it much more difficult by
 saying he wanted to

01:16:50.040 --> 01:16:55.709
speak at 10 a.m.
 And Nicholas might not
 remember this. But Nicholas

01:16:55.710 --> 01:17:01.309
said, no, no one is going
 to come at 10 a.m. 4 p.m.
 Have him come at 4 p.m.

01:17:01.310 --> 01:17:05.079
I said, can you
 come at 4 p.m.? Professor
 Negroponte -- he said, no,

01:17:05.080 --> 01:17:09.319
I'm coming at 10 a.m.
 (sic). If no one is going to
 show up at 4 p.m. (sic),

01:17:09.320 --> 01:17:14.909
I never wanted to talk
 to them anyways. So he
 showed up at 10 a.m.

01:17:14.910 --> 01:17:17.799
And I'm sure
 Nicholas remembers the auditorium
 was packed. It was

01:17:17.800 --> 01:17:21.409
standing room only.
 It was an incredible
 outpouring for Mr. Rand

01:17:21.410 --> 01:17:28.399
and design in general,
 which was exciting. Arrogance.
 I loved that

01:17:28.400 --> 01:17:32.199
moment when she says,
 arrogance. And everyone kind
 of like bristled,

01:17:32.200 --> 01:17:38.929
like, yeah, but there's a
 Bob Sutton book about the
 no assholes. But wait

01:17:38.930 --> 01:17:43.059
a second. I don't
 understand this. Well, it's a
 fact that artists might

01:17:43.060 --> 01:17:48.819
appear arrogant. But as --
 when I ran the School of
 Design, I was able to

01:17:48.820 --> 01:17:53.099
work with many artists. And
 I found this one thing
 I didn't understand at

01:17:53.100 --> 01:17:58.079
the beginning. I used to
 visit all of the lunchrooms
 and bring my food

01:17:58.080 --> 01:18:02.219
around and sit with students
 randomly to see if they
 would let me sit

01:18:02.220 --> 01:18:04.729
with them. Sometimes they
 would say, no, no,
 don't sit with us.

01:18:04.730 --> 01:18:08.179
Sometimes, come own here.
 But one day there
 was this one young

01:18:08.180 --> 01:18:12.289
woman who said, come here.
 Come. I was like, oh,
 no. She wants to sit

01:18:12.290 --> 01:18:15.709
with me So I sat
 with her. And she was
 saying, you know, I'm a

01:18:15.710 --> 01:18:20.419
freshman. Oh, no, complaints,
 it's coming Something is
 coming. I'm a

01:18:20.420 --> 01:18:25.539
freshman. And it was
 really hard that first semester.
 You know, I come

01:18:25.540 --> 01:18:28.789
from the Midwest. This is
 all new to me. I
 didn't understand it. Didn't

01:18:28.790 --> 01:18:33.739
understand the teachers. It was
 just so hard. Every day
 I just didn't know

01:18:33.740 --> 01:18:38.329
what to do. And she
 said, no I'm in the second
 term. And I know where

01:18:38.330 --> 01:18:42.649
things are. I know what
 kind of questions to ask.
 It's all working really

01:18:42.650 --> 01:18:46.969
well. But I have a
 confession to make. I said,
 oh, no, confession. I have

01:18:46.970 --> 01:18:55.769
to confess that I don't
 feel alive. And so I
 remember like walking back to

01:18:55.770 --> 01:18:59.249
my office and the
 elevator opened and my friend
 Jessie, she was the

01:18:59.250 --> 01:19:02.229
Provost at the time.
 She was right there. I
 said, Jessie, an interesting

01:19:02.230 --> 01:19:05.989
thing happened to me.
 I was sitting with this
 young woman. And she

01:19:05.990 --> 01:19:08.769
said this thing. And Jessie
 looked at me and said,
 well, of course. She's

01:19:08.770 --> 01:19:12.449
an artist. An artist
 doesn't feel alive unless they
 feel the struggle. It's

01:19:12.450 --> 01:19:18.329
through that struggle that
 they feel alive. So it
 may look like arrogance.

01:19:18.330 --> 01:19:24.899
But it's that strength to
 be different that I see
 is there. And lastly,

01:19:24.900 --> 01:19:31.259
persistence. What a good one
 to end on. Think about
 it. I mean, when

01:19:31.260 --> 01:19:36.039
you tell like younger people
 today that there was a
 time when there was

01:19:36.040 --> 01:19:41.519
this like dot matrix typeface,
 not like an anti-alias version
 of it, but like 5

01:19:41.520 --> 01:19:48.959
dots by 7 dots was
 all you got. And I'm
 now a Product Manager/Design

01:19:48.960 --> 01:19:52.419
Lead where I'm at a
 company where you tell people,
 do this No, that's

01:19:52.420 --> 01:19:56.449
not the way you have to
 do it. Do it this way.
 It's better. No, I think it's

01:19:56.450 --> 01:20:01.389
good this way. It gives
 me a sense of how easier
 it is now compared to

01:20:01.390 --> 01:20:04.399
when Muriel was able
 to convince people that
 maybe you need this

01:20:04.400 --> 01:20:09.549
thing called Helvetica. Maybe
 it has to be anti-alias
 so it looks much

01:20:09.550 --> 01:20:14.949
more smooth to the
 eye. Pleasurable. So that kind
 of persistence, I can't

01:20:14.950 --> 01:20:19.749
imagine how hard it was
 for her and the team back
 then to do what has

01:20:19.750 --> 01:20:24.139
happened. But look how
 amazing information is today
 on our devices.

01:20:24.140 --> 01:20:33.499
So persistence Good persistence. So
 all I can say now is
 I am at the very

01:20:33.500 --> 01:20:37.799
end And I'm 0:0 seconds
 and I want to leave
 with this thought that

01:20:37.800 --> 01:20:43.269
Muriel was one of the
 great teachers. And all of
 you are teachers in

01:20:43.270 --> 01:20:47.849
different ways, too.
 So please consider
 yourselves as intellectual

01:20:47.850 --> 01:20:52.649
philanthropists. People that can
 give this away to
 other people. Think

01:20:52.650 --> 01:20:56.839
about opportunities. I used to
 raise money so I will
 do a thing for MIT

01:20:56.840 --> 01:21:00.909
Press. If you have not
 bought this book or aren't
 buying it yet, buy many

01:21:00.910 --> 01:21:04.999
for your friends. Because
 it's a way to
 carry Muriel's legacy forward.

01:21:05.000 --> 01:21:08.149
Thank you.

01:21:08.150 --> 01:21:13.189
 (Applause).

01:21:13.190 --> 01:21:29.999
 [Music].

01:21:30.000 --> 01:21:36.139
&gt;&gt; I'm going to
 introduce you guys. So
 give me two seconds

01:21:36.140 --> 01:21:41.769
Because I also have to
 say one other word, which
 you guys may not know.

01:21:41.770 --> 01:21:47.479
But there's apparently a
 new fund that's been
 created by the Wade family

01:21:47.480 --> 01:21:52.139
I believe
 it's Emily and Jeptha
 -- is anybody

01:21:52.140 --> 01:21:56.729
here from the Wade family?
 No? Anyway, it's a new
 fund for the MIT Press

01:21:56.730 --> 01:22:03.459
which will bear Muriel's
 name. And its purpose,
 as I understand it,

01:22:03.460 --> 01:22:09.859
is to help make the
 art and architecture and design
 books easier to do

01:22:09.860 --> 01:22:14.769
and subventure them and do
 whatever. And yours is the
 first. So we not

01:22:14.770 --> 01:22:20.319
only want to hear about
 the book. But you should
 know it's the first of

01:22:20.320 --> 01:22:25.989
the Muriel Cooper fund I
 guess it's a little nepotistic
 for it to be funding

01:22:25.990 --> 01:22:30.489
the Muriel book.
 But at any
 rate, please come.

01:22:30.490 --> 01:22:34.149
 (Applause).

01:22:34.150 --> 01:22:43.329
&gt;&gt; Thank you. Thank
 you, Nicholas. My name
 is Rob Wiesenberger

01:22:43.330 --> 01:22:47.369
This is David
 Reinfurt. We are co-authors
 of this book,

01:22:47.370 --> 01:22:50.949
which we hope
 you'll see soon,
 entitled "Muriel Cooper."

01:22:50.950 --> 01:22:54.069
We just wanted
 to make a
 few very basic

01:22:54.070 --> 01:22:57.969
observations about the
 book, about its structure,
 about the intent

01:22:57.970 --> 01:23:03.849
behind it. Before that
 we wanted to thank the
 MIT Press for its

01:23:03.850 --> 01:23:07.579
enthusiasm about the project,
 for investing in it.
 Thank the Media Lab

01:23:07.580 --> 01:23:11.929
for hosting. And thank so
 many people in this room
 from the family and

01:23:11.930 --> 01:23:17.639
the colleagues and friends
 of Cooper's. The archivists,
 the librarians, all

01:23:17.640 --> 01:23:20.969
of these people who helped
 to make this material come
 alive for us. So

01:23:20.970 --> 01:23:30.819
just a couple of
 words on this book. The
 first observation is pretty

01:23:30.820 --> 01:23:34.919
straightforward about its scale.
 And hopefully you'll appreciate
 it. It's a

01:23:34.920 --> 01:23:39.859
big book. And we were
 really happy to see that,
 this book can stand up

01:23:39.860 --> 01:23:46.209
beside Cooper's two great
 tomes the Bauhaus of
 1969, after which this

01:23:46.210 --> 01:23:52.549
book takes some inspiration
 visually. And "Learning from
 Las Vegas" of

01:23:52.550 --> 01:23:58.939
1972, which is mercifully back
 in print. So we really
 like that this book

01:23:58.940 --> 01:24:03.609
can hold its own.
 This book dedicated to
 Cooper. Beside those two

01:24:03.610 --> 01:24:05.969
great tomes and this book
 is also about the person
 who helped take us

01:24:05.970 --> 01:24:10.069
beyond the book so
 it feels appropriate. When you
 get into the book,

01:24:10.070 --> 01:24:18.959
you'll see that the epigraph
 to start off with is
 a sort of confession of

01:24:18.960 --> 01:24:26.609
Cooper's. And she says, I
 guess I'm never sure that
 print is truly linear. I

01:24:26.610 --> 01:24:31.339
guess I'm never sure that
 print is truly linear. And
 she said this in the

01:24:31.340 --> 01:24:35.359
'90s when she's already working
 in the digital realm. And
 to us, it seems

01:24:35.360 --> 01:24:40.199
like a nice way to
 start a book. Because it's
 kind of a statement of

01:24:40.200 --> 01:24:45.109
ambivalence. It's a statement
 very typically of Cooper's.
 It's a statement

01:24:45.110 --> 01:24:48.379
of ambivalence. It's a
 statement of possibility, as
 well. She's just gotten

01:24:48.380 --> 01:24:51.999
done of explaining the
 specificity of the printed
 book versus the

01:24:52.000 --> 01:24:55.929
possibilities of the digital.
 And then she back tracks
 and says, actually I

01:24:55.930 --> 01:25:00.529
guess I'm never sure that
 print is truly linear. And
 so it seemed like an

01:25:00.530 --> 01:25:05.739
appropriately self-reflexive way to
 start a book But
 also appropriate to

01:25:05.740 --> 01:25:10.979
her, who felt, even as
 she moved into the digital
 world, that the book

01:25:10.980 --> 01:25:16.089
was a specific kind of
 technology. The Codex book had
 its own set of

01:25:16.090 --> 01:25:22.689
possibilities. And simultaneity for
 her versus linear, she
 really strove for

01:25:22.690 --> 01:25:27.339
simultaneity. Not that you start
 here and end here. But
 that in all of her,

01:25:27.340 --> 01:25:31.779
whether it was a highly
 layered image that comes at
 you all at once or

01:25:31.780 --> 01:25:35.449
can be accessed in different
 ways or a video disk
 that you can skip

01:25:35.450 --> 01:25:39.109
around based on your own
 path or the hyperlinking of
 the world we now

01:25:39.110 --> 01:25:46.759
live in, simultaneity non-linear
 was so important to
 Cooper. And I think

01:25:46.760 --> 01:25:50.549
she showed books can do
 that. And so we wanted
 to start with this very

01:25:50.550 --> 01:25:57.499
equivocal and self-reflexive
 acknowledgement of the
 power that books

01:25:57.500 --> 01:26:04.379
still actually hold that
 she showed to us. Very
 sort of basic observation.

01:26:04.380 --> 01:26:10.609
But in the Table of
 Contents, we separated out --
 there's a text portion

01:26:10.610 --> 01:26:14.288
and an image portion. And that's
the structure of the Bauhaus book

01:26:14.289 --> 01:26:18.019
that Cooper designed, as well. She
inherited it from this German

01:26:18.020 --> 01:26:21.419
predecessor. But we both
 wrote essays on both her
 early and late work

01:26:21.420 --> 01:26:25.329
respectively, her work in
 print and software respectively.
 And they are

01:26:25.330 --> 01:26:28.989
essays, they are attempts,
 they are brief. And then
 we show you the

01:26:28.990 --> 01:26:33.049
work. And we really
 wanted to foreground the work
 and let people enter

01:26:33.050 --> 01:26:37.789
in either direction. And
 we think that, you know,
 at different times maybe

01:26:37.790 --> 01:26:41.089
the text will be
 galvanizing for people, her words
 At other times, the

01:26:41.090 --> 01:26:44.297
images will be. At other times still
the images may feel dated and then

01:26:44.298 --> 01:26:47.459
they will feel new again. So we
wanted to provide these two ways of

01:26:47.460 --> 01:26:52.399
accessing it And you'll see
 there are these two texts
 and then there are

01:26:52.400 --> 01:26:57.479
these three sections
 We call them portfolio
 sections And they

01:26:57.480 --> 01:27:02.919
respectively, they represent her
 work. They are design
 and teaching and

01:27:02.920 --> 01:27:08.649
research. And they were
 successive categories in her
 career. She did

01:27:08.650 --> 01:27:11.909
them one after another.
 Of course they bleed into
 one another. They are

01:27:11.910 --> 01:27:15.229
not such crisp distinctions.
 But the point that I
 wanted to make that

01:27:15.230 --> 01:27:20.399
struck me -- that
 struck us about Cooper was
 that design and teaching

01:27:20.400 --> 01:27:24.569
and research, we didn't
 write those categories. That she
 did. And I think

01:27:24.570 --> 01:27:28.929
we have heard tonight
 that her career intersected
 with a lot of

01:27:28.930 --> 01:27:33.249
interesting people It's actually
 Paul Rand, who we
 just heard about, who

01:27:33.250 --> 01:27:36.039
recommended her for a job
 at the MIT Press. It's,
 you know, the great

01:27:36.040 --> 01:27:39.969
design luminary Georgia
 Kalpesh (phonetic) before
 that who brought

01:27:39.970 --> 01:27:42.659
her onto MIT. She
 designed the Bauhaus book
 when Herbert Bayer, the

01:27:42.660 --> 01:27:45.676
great Bauhaus master, wasn't
available. She was at
the MIT Media Lab

01:27:45.677 --> 01:27:48.909
with a whole set of luminaries, many
of whom are in this room. And yet

01:27:48.910 --> 01:27:54.519
she designed and redesigned
 her own career in
 really interesting ways.

01:27:54.520 --> 01:27:59.669
So these chapters, design
 and teaching and research,
 we quote her in

01:27:59.670 --> 01:28:02.809
the book as saying, I
 very deliberately decided I no
 longer wanted to be

01:28:02.810 --> 01:28:06.509
a designer in a
 traditional sense. And she
 uses the word deliberately

01:28:06.510 --> 01:28:11.389
about three times in a
 sentence or two And that
 idea of the deliberate,

01:28:11.390 --> 01:28:14.759
the purposive, the intentional,
 that she was forging
 her way, creating

01:28:14.760 --> 01:28:20.569
careers in fields that simply
 didn't exist with a lot
 of assists from the

01:28:20.570 --> 01:28:26.329
brilliant minds around her.
 But that she wrote
 these chapters. So the

01:28:26.330 --> 01:28:34.459
last thing I wanted to
 say is, you know, it's striking
 when you hear about --

01:28:34.460 --> 01:28:40.129
when we hear about
 Muriel tonight, I mean,
 we didn't know her

01:28:40.130 --> 01:28:44.319
personally. And we're very aware
 of that. I mean, I
 was 10 when she

01:28:44.320 --> 01:28:50.799
died. You will --
 David will tell his passing
 connection. But we didn't

01:28:50.800 --> 01:28:55.549
know her. And we respect
 all of the help of those
 who did. And we want

01:28:55.550 --> 01:28:58.679
to provide this way
 in. And it's striking
 that she's simultaneously by

01:28:58.680 --> 01:29:02.449
everyone's account this
 kind of firecracker,
 this incredible personality.

01:29:02.450 --> 01:29:06.389
So idiosyncratic and yet is
 not well known and didn't
 really have the time

01:29:06.390 --> 01:29:10.609
in her own life
 because she was working so
 hard. Often behind the

01:29:10.610 --> 01:29:14.919
scenes. To really consolidate her
 work in a clear way
 and say, this is my

01:29:14.920 --> 01:29:19.569
career. And it probably
 wasn't even in her character
 to talk about herself

01:29:19.570 --> 01:29:23.839
or her work in that
 way to her great credit. So
 this book is that attempt

01:29:23.840 --> 01:29:28.709
to, with a little distance,
 to consolidate her career and
 look at it with

01:29:28.710 --> 01:29:37.939
some perspective. So we hope
 you enjoy it. And with
 that I wanted to

01:29:37.940 --> 01:29:40.569
turn it over to David.

01:29:40.570 --> 01:29:46.699
 (Applause).

01:29:46.700 --> 01:29:50.349
&gt;&gt; Okay. I did
 not know Muriel Cooper, either,
 as Rob made clear.

01:29:50.350 --> 01:29:54.995
I did, however, in I think it
was January of 1995, I was

01:29:54.996 --> 01:29:59.640
a young designer working in
New York, not knowing what I

01:30:00.000 --> 01:30:04.159
was doing. And I thought
 it would be fantastic to
 come and study at the

01:30:04.160 --> 01:30:08.839
Media Lab and I arranged
 to make a visit up here
 in January of 1995 to

01:30:08.840 --> 01:30:15.569
visit the VLW. And at
 that point, I saw some
 of the work on screen.

01:30:15.570 --> 01:30:20.159
Actually Lisa Strausfeld was
 a student here at the
 time. And I remember

01:30:20.160 --> 01:30:25.169
thinking it was fantastic
 that she was an architect
 before. But when I

01:30:25.170 --> 01:30:29.649
saw this work, then the
 next time I ended up
 not coming back to

01:30:29.650 --> 01:30:34.419
Graduate School because I went
 to go work at IDO
 in San Francisco. In

01:30:34.420 --> 01:30:38.739
1995. And I went
 to work for Geita Solomon
 (phonetic), who was a

01:30:38.740 --> 01:30:43.939
student from the Media
 Lab and who had
 finished And what was

01:30:43.940 --> 01:30:47.599
striking at the time out
 in San Francisco, I was
 hired as an interaction

01:30:47.600 --> 01:30:51.789
designer which was quite
 a new field at that
 time, Muriel Cooper's work,

01:30:51.790 --> 01:30:57.609
and specifically the
 information landscapes, were
 presented as kind of

01:30:57.610 --> 01:31:03.639
primary material as I
 was starting to understand
 what might happen on

01:31:03.640 --> 01:31:14.539
a screen and its relationship
 to design So that was
 soon -- not so long

01:31:14.540 --> 01:31:19.889
after the TEDTalk in
 Monterey, which we have
 heard described a couple

01:31:19.890 --> 01:31:23.869
of times tonight from
 people who were there
 and who organized it.

01:31:23.870 --> 01:31:31.139
Anyway, when I first
 saw this -- saw the
 information landscapes work, it

01:31:31.140 --> 01:31:34.759
was first as printed images
 in an article by Janet
 Abrams in an I.D.

01:31:34.760 --> 01:31:42.389
Magazine I think. And I
 later saw it moving not
 very long after that. And

01:31:42.390 --> 01:31:48.399
I was struck as
 a designer with the way
 in which familiar typographic

01:31:48.400 --> 01:31:52.739
relationships, which conveyed
 meaning, which usually
 were done with

01:31:52.740 --> 01:31:56.539
bold or size or
 two dimensional approaches, seemed
 to be completely

01:31:56.540 --> 01:32:00.639
intact but just another
 dimension was added to it.
 And of course that's

01:32:00.640 --> 01:32:03.819
a third dimension. But
 it's also a dimension
 of time more

01:32:03.820 --> 01:32:07.609
consequentially here. And I
 recognized this as work
 that was happening

01:32:07.610 --> 01:32:10.739
on the screen that opened
 up a whole new set
 of possibilities. But I

01:32:10.740 --> 01:32:15.919
recognized it coming specifically
 from like a design
 place. It was about

01:32:15.920 --> 01:32:23.299
ten years before I
 came into contact with her
 work again. And during

01:32:23.300 --> 01:32:29.819
that time, I was shocked that
 a lot of the -- a lot
 of this work I didn't hear

01:32:29.820 --> 01:32:36.589
discussed within graphic
 design or interaction
 design. Extensively

01:32:36.590 --> 01:32:39.409
during that time period
 Maybe it was my
 own removed from whatever

01:32:39.410 --> 01:32:42.289
scenes were talking about it.
 But it was just absent.
 And in 2005, I came

01:32:42.290 --> 01:32:45.969
back to MIT and I
 was doing some graphic design
 work with the Center

01:32:45.970 --> 01:32:50.919
for Advanced Visual Studies, which
 was at the end of
 its kind of run.

01:32:50.920 --> 01:32:54.159
And I stumbled into
 a closest up there, which
 was filled with posters.

01:32:54.160 --> 01:32:58.659
They were just stacked
 one on top of
 each other. Absolutely fabulous, f

01:32:58.660 --> 01:33:02.449
antastic things Very excited
 to find these things.
 Started to pull them

01:33:02.450 --> 01:33:08.919
out. And at the same time
 I found a -- like a
 CV from Muriel Cooper at

01:33:08.920 --> 01:33:14.299
the Center for Advanced
 Visual Studies from I
 think 1971. And I

01:33:14.300 --> 01:33:20.679
expected that these posters
 were likely designed by
 Muriel Cooper. I

01:33:20.680 --> 01:33:25.489
was quickly corrected as
 many of them weren't.
 But what I recognized

01:33:25.490 --> 01:33:28.549
in these was a similar
 approach to what I had
 seen in the information

01:33:28.550 --> 01:33:32.389
landscapes, which seemed to
 be a kind of
 deep investment in the

01:33:32.390 --> 01:33:36.109
temporal aspect of making
 a piece of graphic
 design work. So whether

01:33:36.110 --> 01:33:40.579
it's the rotations that are
 in the print on the left,
 which use the kind of

01:33:40.580 --> 01:33:45.709
staging of a printing press
 as a way to generate
 form or on the right,

01:33:45.710 --> 01:33:49.579
which is made with a
 split fountain, which is another
 way of kind of

01:33:49.580 --> 01:33:58.899
using the tools to
 produce the graphic effect
 essentially. Now, in the

01:33:58.900 --> 01:34:02.569
book, these are several
 spreads from the book. In
 the book, we've made

01:34:02.570 --> 01:34:07.629
a real attempt to
 show the things that we
 found, pieces of Muriel

01:34:07.630 --> 01:34:11.289
Cooper's work, and her
 students' work and ancillary
 materials as much

01:34:11.290 --> 01:34:15.499
as artifacts as possible.
 So you see something
 like the mechanical

01:34:15.500 --> 01:34:19.869
artwork for the MIT
 Press logo, which is
 presented with the masking

01:34:19.870 --> 01:34:24.439
tape on it. And the
 sketch -- the original sketch that
 looks like it led to

01:34:24.440 --> 01:34:29.699
this proposal for a logo
 on the left. These are
 largely left unframed by

01:34:29.700 --> 01:34:34.319
kind of writing essentially.
 And the idea as Rob
 described, as well, was

01:34:34.320 --> 01:34:38.339
to make this a kind
 of source book for other
 people to continue this

01:34:38.340 --> 01:34:43.529
work. It seemed --
 given our distance from the
 material and the real

01:34:43.530 --> 01:34:48.459
situation, it also seemed
 like the most productive
 positive thing we

01:34:48.460 --> 01:34:51.949
could do was present this
 work so that other people
 can take up the

01:34:51.950 --> 01:34:56.179
project. So that other
 people can get excited
 about this singular

01:34:56.180 --> 01:35:06.279
collection of design --
 of design. And continue
 it forward, whether

01:35:06.280 --> 01:35:10.589
through research or taking
 ideas, et cetera. Now, you
 know, it gets sewn

01:35:10.590 --> 01:35:17.519
up in this giant --
 in this rather large book
 which is very beautifully

01:35:17.520 --> 01:35:23.389
produced by the MIT Press.
 We're thrilled with the way
 that it came out.

01:35:23.390 --> 01:35:29.189
But on the spine of
 this book is -- down in
 the bottom, you know, you

01:35:29.190 --> 01:35:33.989
don't have to see --
 is a piece of Muriel Cooper's
 work that we all know

01:35:33.990 --> 01:35:39.159
well. But seen in situ,
 not removed. So this would have
 to be one of my

01:35:39.160 --> 01:35:42.789
favorite pieces of graphic
 design. And the way
 in which it oscillates

01:35:42.790 --> 01:35:46.619
between a kind
 of figurative typographic thing
 and machine readable

01:35:46.620 --> 01:35:49.579
graphic. But seeing it do
 the type of work it's
 meant to do, which is

01:35:49.580 --> 01:35:52.789
identify this book as MIT
 Press. And I just think we
 -- both of us feel

01:35:52.790 --> 01:35:56.909
quite honored to have
 it published by MIT,
 given the subject matter

01:35:56.910 --> 01:36:03.249
that's in it. So we hope
 you'll look at it. Enjoy it.
 And make some work from it.

01:36:03.250 --> 01:36:09.959
 (Applause).

01:36:09.960 --> 01:36:38.119
 [Music]

01:36:38.120 --> 01:36:42.719
 (Applause).

01:36:42.720 --> 01:36:53.519
&gt;&gt; Hello, everybody, I'm
 Todd Machover. And we
 have a wonderful panel

01:36:53.520 --> 01:36:56.889
here to talk
 about Muriel as
 a teacher.

01:36:56.890 --> 01:37:00.199
And I think we'll
 have an opportunity to
 talk about the culture

01:37:00.200 --> 01:37:03.779
of the Visible Language
 Workshop, as well. We
 have Ron McNeill, who

01:37:03.780 --> 01:37:08.389
was really Muriel's partner
 for the entire time
 that the Visible Language

01:37:08.390 --> 01:37:12.509
Workshop ran. And really
 ran it together. So we're
 going to hear from

01:37:12.510 --> 01:37:16.219
Ron about how it started
 and what it meant and
 how it worked Lisa

01:37:16.220 --> 01:37:20.049
Strausfeld, who was mentioned,
 started out as an
 architect but came

01:37:20.050 --> 01:37:23.169
and studied design with
 Muriel in the Visible
 Language Workshop as

01:37:23.170 --> 01:37:26.999
one of the
 world's most prominent designers.
 Worked with Pentagram

01:37:27.000 --> 01:37:29.679
independently and now is
 at the New York
 University. And David Small,

01:37:29.680 --> 01:37:35.139
who, when he started working
 with Muriel, told me just
 before -- I hadn't

01:37:35.140 --> 01:37:37.829
remembered this -- hadn't
 done any design work at
 all and found his

01:37:37.830 --> 01:37:41.429
way to Muriel through
 photography and became a
 designer in Muriel's

01:37:41.430 --> 01:37:48.059
tutelage, started a small
 design firm which is
 one of the most

01:37:48.060 --> 01:37:51.049
prominent design firms in the
 world. So we'll talk to
 them in just a

01:37:51.050 --> 01:37:53.939
second. I just wanted to
 make a couple of comments
 first. One is that I

01:37:53.940 --> 01:37:59.369
was one of the very
 first people to be hired
 at the Media Lab. People

01:37:59.370 --> 01:38:02.859
like Muriel and Nicholas
 were at MIT before the
 Media Lab. But when

01:38:02.860 --> 01:38:06.639
the building opened, I was
 one of the first people
 to come here. And

01:38:06.640 --> 01:38:10.939
Muriel was one of
 the very kindest people to
 everybody but I remember

01:38:10.940 --> 01:38:15.809
certainly to me. I came
 here. I was 30 years old
 I think. I was a

01:38:15.810 --> 01:38:20.499
musician, an artist. And
 Muriel understood that it wasn't
 to easy to be

01:38:20.500 --> 01:38:25.379
an artist in a culture,
 even the Media Lab. But a
 culture like MIT. So she

01:38:25.380 --> 01:38:28.829
never said too much about
 it. But she always found
 time to check in

01:38:28.830 --> 01:38:32.739
just to see how I was
 doing. To see if things were
 okay. And to see if

01:38:32.740 --> 01:38:37.079
that part of my
 creative work here was being
 nurtured She really cared.

01:38:37.080 --> 01:38:41.849
And now one of the things
 that I'm proudest of of all
 of the things that I

01:38:41.850 --> 01:38:45.299
do is I'm the Muriel
 Cooper professor here at MIT.
 And not a day goes

01:38:45.300 --> 01:38:49.219
by when I don't think
 about somehow trying to live
 up to her example as

01:38:49.220 --> 01:38:57.779
a designer and an artist
 and as a mentor. And
 as somebody who helps

01:38:57.780 --> 01:39:02.599
to shape this place. And
 as a human being. Because
 she really was as

01:39:02.600 --> 01:39:07.279
close to the soul of
 the Media Lab as anybody. And
 I'll just tell you one

01:39:07.280 --> 01:39:11.179
little story before I turn
 it over to our panelists.
 Ron will recognize this.

01:39:11.180 --> 01:39:15.149
But you two won't. It has
 to do with her as a
 teacher and as a mentor.

01:39:15.150 --> 01:39:19.249
So in the early days
 of the Media Lab, the
 place was small enough that

01:39:19.250 --> 01:39:22.899
when we would go
 through the admissions process
 to pick our

01:39:22.900 --> 01:39:26.649
students, we would actually all
 sit around a big table.
 We would do this

01:39:26.650 --> 01:39:29.759
a few times iteratively. And
 all of the folders for
 all of the students

01:39:29.760 --> 01:39:33.399
would be on the table.
 And the place was small
 enough that we would

01:39:33.400 --> 01:39:36.859
all read the folders
 for everybody. And we would
 pass them around the

01:39:36.860 --> 01:39:39.899
table. And the idea was
 we would have three or
 four of these meetings.

01:39:39.900 --> 01:39:45.529
And each of the
 meetings you were supposed to
 make a shorter and

01:39:45.530 --> 01:39:48.889
shorter list. You would
 read the folders and
 you're supposed to say,

01:39:48.890 --> 01:39:51.899
okay, there are 50 people
 who want to be in my
 group and now my job

01:39:51.900 --> 01:39:54.429
is to figure out the 30
 And now it's the 10. And
 then -- and every time we

01:39:54.430 --> 01:39:57.759
would make a list on
 the board. And Nicholas, you
 know, runs a pretty

01:39:57.760 --> 01:40:01.959
tight ship. And he would
 make sure that every time
 we would kind of

01:40:01.960 --> 01:40:05.649
whittle down the list. We
 would even write the names
 on the board of

01:40:05.650 --> 01:40:09.639
our short list. It's an
 important thing to do. One
 of the biggest things is

01:40:09.640 --> 01:40:13.589
to pick the right students
 here. I'll never forget, all
 of us, we wanted to

01:40:13.590 --> 01:40:17.319
follow what Nicholas wanted
 us to do. So we
 would whittle down our

01:40:17.320 --> 01:40:20.729
lists And we would get
 to the second, third --
 we would get to the

01:40:20.730 --> 01:40:27.179
finalist. And here are the
 two people I really want
 to admit. And we

01:40:27.180 --> 01:40:31.999
would get to Muriel
 and say, okay, Muriel, what's
 your short list? She

01:40:32.000 --> 01:40:36.069
would say, well, I've
 got 20 people. Nicholas would
 go, what, 20 people,

01:40:36.070 --> 01:40:39.819
no. I need more time.
 Just 20 people. So part
 of it was the Chinese

01:40:39.820 --> 01:40:44.319
menu thing about maybe not
 being able to decide but
 I really think it's

01:40:44.320 --> 01:40:47.989
because she took so
 much time to think about
 every single one of

01:40:47.990 --> 01:40:53.619
those applicants And to
 figure out where there
 was something special

01:40:53.620 --> 01:40:57.499
which might not be so
 obvious in the normal way
 of judging a student

01:40:57.500 --> 01:41:00.929
and to figure out not
 only who she wanted to
 nurture but what kind of

01:41:00.930 --> 01:41:04.029
group she could bring
 together to make the
 Visible Language Workshop

01:41:04.030 --> 01:41:07.429
special. It was really
 amazing. It drove Nicholas crazy.
 But she was also

01:41:07.430 --> 01:41:11.569
the only one I think
 it's because you were such
 close friends but also

01:41:11.570 --> 01:41:14.259
you respected each other so
 much she was the only
 one he would let

01:41:14.260 --> 01:41:18.139
get away with that. But
 she always did. But here
 are the kind of people

01:41:18.140 --> 01:41:22.249
she admitted. I would like
 to maybe start with Ron.
 Just -- because you

01:41:22.250 --> 01:41:26.609
were here at the
 very beginning. Tell us
 how the Visible Language

01:41:26.610 --> 01:41:29.169
Workshop started Why
 it started. How
 it worked.

01:41:29.170 --> 01:41:31.789
&gt;&gt; I thought that
 it would be nice
 to see the very,

01:41:31.790 --> 01:41:37.359
very grubby beginning of
 her third act where
 we started the VLW.

01:41:37.360 --> 01:41:52.209
So the VLW's story
 starts in the early 1970s.
 Way before the Internet.

01:41:52.210 --> 01:41:56.399
Way before the
 PC. With me, a
 photographer turned print maker.

01:41:56.400 --> 01:42:00.549
In my work space
 on the 4th floor of Building
 5 in the Department of

01:42:00.550 --> 01:42:06.309
Architecture. Slating over
 a hot offset press.
 Teaching a class

01:42:06.310 --> 01:42:14.129
in non-silver
 image making called
 photographics. So

01:42:14.130 --> 01:42:21.219
before I met her, Muriel
 and I shared a friend,
 Tom Norton. Who told her

01:42:21.220 --> 01:42:25.829
about my offset press. And
 as it turned out, Muriel
 was looking to start

01:42:25.830 --> 01:42:35.269
ACT III of her career
 outside of MIT Press. So
 you might imagine that

01:42:35.270 --> 01:42:40.759
Muriel was visualizing something
 like this when Tom
 told her about my

01:42:40.760 --> 01:42:46.789
Press. But of course
 you would be wrong. Because
 that's what she was

01:42:46.790 --> 01:42:53.949
imagining The scene of
 a group of eager,
 very smart future designer

01:42:53.950 --> 01:42:58.979
disrupters with Muriel as
 the lioness den mother
 teaching them in the

01:42:58.980 --> 01:43:10.459
ways of disruption. After
 she came on board
 officially what Muriel soon

01:43:10.460 --> 01:43:14.789
created was an
 iconic humanities distribution
 class called messages

01:43:14.790 --> 01:43:18.689
and means. It
 was consistently oversubscribed
 because it asked

01:43:18.690 --> 01:43:21.989
students who normally spent
 all day thinking abstractly
 to connect their

01:43:21.990 --> 01:43:26.839
imagination and their
 hands in creative exploration.
 And of course,

01:43:26.840 --> 01:43:33.369
many of them had never,
 ever even tried to do
 that before. So Muriel felt

01:43:33.370 --> 01:43:38.809
strongly that the author
 and maker should be the
 same person. Or more

01:43:38.810 --> 01:43:43.139
practically, back then, that
 the gap between the
 author and the maker

01:43:43.140 --> 01:43:47.539
should be collapsed as
 much as possible. And in
 search of the closest

01:43:47.540 --> 01:43:52.869
thing to an interactive
 experience possible, Muriel threw
 out all of the

01:43:52.870 --> 01:43:57.079
then conventions of reproduction
 technology to get us
 as close to a

01:43:57.080 --> 01:44:01.039
hands-on interactive design
 production experience as
 was possible.

01:44:01.040 --> 01:44:08.739
Often going from concept
 to mask to plate to
 printed piece in one

01:44:08.740 --> 01:44:16.659
class. So she called
 this iconic class project
 a rotation. Where the

01:44:16.660 --> 01:44:21.859
students would use square
 paper, create the mask
 by working directly

01:44:21.860 --> 01:44:27.649
on acetate with press type.
 But use only one plate
 and rotate the paper

01:44:27.650 --> 01:44:38.769
between color changes. Notice
 the female press operator.
 So sadly very

01:44:38.770 --> 01:44:44.549
few of these pieces
 have actually survived. And the
 ones that I'm going

01:44:44.550 --> 01:44:49.029
to show you are not
 fabulous. But, you know, they
 are still I think worth

01:44:49.030 --> 01:44:57.719
seeing. Here is the
 split fountain. Very popular. The
 third color. Here is

01:44:57.720 --> 01:45:11.379
another approach. And yet
 another. So she also
 inherited a basic visual

01:45:11.380 --> 01:45:16.399
literacy freshman seminar
 called creative seeing. She
 had 3 assistants

01:45:16.400 --> 01:45:21.049
in a class of
 60 bright-eyed bushy-tailed
students. And here they are

01:45:21.050 --> 01:45:31.949
taking part in a sky
 art event So here they
 are mapping their physical,

01:45:31.950 --> 01:45:39.299
social and family
 relationships. And then
 below exploring instant

01:45:39.300 --> 01:45:45.369
reproduction technologies. And
 of course they explored
 the book form.

01:45:45.370 --> 01:45:54.369
Just a few examples
 of probably an incredibly
 varied class. So our

01:45:54.370 --> 01:45:59.429
computational explorations
started in earnest when I got
 a grant to

01:45:59.430 --> 01:46:05.589
build a billboard painting
 machine. Which you see
 here. It worked well

01:46:05.590 --> 01:46:09.609
for about an afternoon
(Chuckles)

01:46:09.610 --> 01:46:13.609
But the part
 of the project
 that really

01:46:13.610 --> 01:46:16.789
excited both Muriel and
 me was the interactive
 color computer graphic

01:46:16.790 --> 01:46:21.639
image and text editing
 system that was needed in
 order to create the

01:46:21.640 --> 01:46:28.959
images for the
 billboard painter. So after
 a three-year collaboration

01:46:28.960 --> 01:46:33.149
between many grad and
 undergrad assistants, we had
 one of the first

01:46:33.150 --> 01:46:39.899
artist workstations capable
 of synthesizing image and
 type in near real-time.

01:46:39.900 --> 01:46:46.219
Sadly the crude
 image type handling capability
 was a constant

01:46:46.220 --> 01:46:52.429
frustration for Muriel. But
 it was also extremely hard
 to fix because the

01:46:52.430 --> 01:47:00.349
CPU that we were using
 was about as fast as
 the fast MacIntosh. So

01:47:00.350 --> 01:47:07.059
there was a time just
 before -- sort of the
 middle of the project, just

01:47:07.060 --> 01:47:11.219
about every morning I would
 come in and see a
 new function or a new

01:47:11.220 --> 01:47:15.219
image added by one
 of the student artists that
 were informed by work

01:47:15.220 --> 01:47:19.949
by other students that
 had been done previous
 days. The 300 megabyte

01:47:19.950 --> 01:47:25.749
image disk space was
 a big canvas that let
 everybody else share ideas

01:47:25.750 --> 01:47:34.479
in a free and easy
 way. Of course Muriel was not
 off limits as a subject.

01:47:34.480 --> 01:47:42.429
So when we first moved
 into the Media Lab, the
 tools were still just as

01:47:42.430 --> 01:47:47.429
frustrating for Muriel as
 ever. But things began to
 change for the good

01:47:47.430 --> 01:47:55.809
when David Small, Suguru
 Ishizaki and Mrs. Strausfeld
 and earlier Renn Sen

01:47:55.810 --> 01:48:02.649
(phonetic) began the
 information landscape work.
 Over to Dave.

01:48:02.650 --> 01:48:07.309
&gt;&gt; Okay David, do you
 want to take it from
 there? That's a good introduction

01:48:07.310 --> 01:48:09.909
 (Applause).

01:48:09.910 --> 01:48:14.159
&gt;&gt; Thank you. So
 as I think has
 been mentioned multiple times,

01:48:14.160 --> 01:48:20.889
I was involved in that
 famous couple of days in
 Monterey at the TED5

01:48:20.890 --> 01:48:26.849
conference. And I
 was that waiter
 that Nicholas described

01:48:26.850 --> 01:48:30.369
that I thought we
 were going to go
 to Monterey, we

01:48:30.370 --> 01:48:33.359
were going to speak on
 the second day and then
 was going to see Big

01:48:33.360 --> 01:48:37.979
Sur and go to the
 aquarium. It was going to
 be like a fantastic thing.

01:48:37.980 --> 01:48:43.149
Instead Muriel angled from
 like the moment we landed
 to get us almost

01:48:43.150 --> 01:48:48.559
like the very last spot
 on the schedule. And we
 spent those three days,

01:48:48.560 --> 01:48:53.779
me hunched over the
 computer, and her changing
 her mind over and

01:48:53.780 --> 01:49:00.069
over and over. So if we
 can switch to the -- to
 my phone here. One thing

01:49:00.070 --> 01:49:05.209
that's always bothered me
 about that piece is we
 really didn't have at

01:49:05.210 --> 01:49:09.379
the time any way of
 documenting it except to make
 a kind of videotape

01:49:09.380 --> 01:49:16.459
which was always so
 like fuzzy and wasn't alive.
 And two years ago

01:49:16.460 --> 01:49:20.589
when the Museum of
 Modern Art acquired this
 piece for their

01:49:20.590 --> 01:49:23.699
collection, I said, what
 do you mean acquire the
 piece? You want what?

01:49:23.700 --> 01:49:27.899
What is it? They said,
 well, I guess the videotape.
 I said, okay, but maybe

01:49:27.900 --> 01:49:33.039
we can do more. And
 there's a group here at the
 Media Lab -- there's a

01:49:33.040 --> 01:49:36.299
lot of different research
 groups. But there's a
 group called Nexus which

01:49:36.300 --> 01:49:40.289
manages the network and all
 of the computers. And I
 wrote to them, I

01:49:40.290 --> 01:49:45.579
said, do you have our
 backups from 23 years ago?
 They were like, oh,

01:49:45.580 --> 01:49:51.379
no, they are online. I'll
 just email you. So all
 of the original source code

01:49:51.380 --> 01:49:55.429
was there. It's still
 there. And I inveigled my
 husband to port everything

01:49:55.430 --> 01:50:00.719
to the phone over the
 past couple of weeks. So this
 is a facsimile of the

01:50:00.720 --> 01:50:10.019
presentation that was given
 at TED from the
 original source code and

01:50:10.020 --> 01:50:17.099
this is unlike -- unlike
 the videotape this is really
 what it felt like. So

01:50:17.100 --> 01:50:20.889
imagine this. I can see
 it from this angle and I
 can see it from this

01:50:20.890 --> 01:50:24.269
angle. But then you
 have Muriel over your shoulder
 saying, should it be

01:50:24.270 --> 01:50:27.119
learning and common
 sense? Should it be
 . . .

01:50:27.120 --> 01:50:28.989
 (Chuckles).

01:50:28.990 --> 01:50:31.579
&gt;&gt; I don't know
 about that color. It was
 like changing over and over

01:50:31.580 --> 01:50:34.749
and over again up
 until like an hour or
 two before we went on stage.

01:50:34.750 --> 01:50:38.729
It was like
 one of the most terrifying
 things in my life.

01:50:38.730 --> 01:50:42.179
And then she talked I had
 no idea going into it what
 she was going to say.

01:50:42.180 --> 01:50:47.489
She never made a note
 or jotted anything down of what
 she was going to say.

01:50:47.490 --> 01:50:50.599
She just talked and
 I latched onto everything that
 she said as much as

01:50:50.600 --> 01:50:54.119
I could
 and tried to keep
 up with her.

01:50:54.120 --> 01:50:59.969
So anyway, just --
 I just wanted to
 -- I didn't want

01:50:59.970 --> 01:51:03.349
people to not have sort
 of a feel for what this
 actually was So the end

01:51:03.350 --> 01:51:08.469
of the little story, hopefully
 not the end-end of the
 story but the end for

01:51:08.470 --> 01:51:12.309
today was that we
 submitted it to the Apple
 App Store last night

01:51:12.310 --> 01:51:15.659
because I was hoping I
 could tell everyone to go
 to the App Store and

01:51:15.660 --> 01:51:21.609
download it. And they
 kicked it out. They
 said it had insufficient

01:51:21.610 --> 01:51:26.259
functionality. Or -- sorry;
 minimal functionality. I'm like,
 yeah, it doesn't

01:51:26.260 --> 01:51:28.409
do anything.

01:51:28.410 --> 01:51:31.859
 (Chuckles).

01:51:31.860 --> 01:51:34.259
&gt;&gt; But yeah, so
 it's just about going down
 and down, tighter and tighter.

01:51:34.260 --> 01:51:38.149
And then this
 idea, too, if you could
 look at something one way

01:51:38.150 --> 01:51:42.199
but then maybe
 you could look at
 it a different way.

01:51:42.200 --> 01:51:44.799
You could go back to this
 way and then this way. So
 to me, I just sat

01:51:44.800 --> 01:51:48.519
there for weeks flipping back
 and forth like, now I
 can read this. The

01:51:48.520 --> 01:51:54.389
other thing is still on the
 screen. But now I can read
 that. But now I can

01:51:54.390 --> 01:51:58.799
read this. And it was
 just like one of the most
 exciting times of my life

01:51:58.800 --> 01:52:04.559
to have been involved in
 this. And I'm just happy to
 be able to share it

01:52:04.560 --> 01:52:08.639
with everyone here.

01:52:08.640 --> 01:52:11.149
 (Applause).

01:52:11.150 --> 01:52:16.169
&gt;&gt; Lisa?

01:52:16.170 --> 01:52:19.909
&gt;&gt; Thanks. I'm going to
 start with a little detail.
 So when we got those

01:52:19.910 --> 01:52:24.029
silicon graphics workstations
 at the Media
 Lab, which happened

01:52:24.030 --> 01:52:30.309
just after I arrived,
 they came with built-in fonts.
 And they were awful.

01:52:30.310 --> 01:52:39.109
But they also in
 3D -- I think all
 of them had thickness.

01:52:39.110 --> 01:52:43.609
Like you were almost
 sort of required to
 use a Z

01:52:43.610 --> 01:52:48.279
dimension. And I think,
 you know, implying that
 the software developers

01:52:48.280 --> 01:52:54.849
from SGI were focused on
 big extruded text. And one
 of the things -- I

01:52:54.850 --> 01:53:00.819
think it's an important
 detail. Because the way those
 fonts were built by

01:53:00.820 --> 01:53:09.379
Dave and the team
 was really not something
 that was really designed

01:53:09.380 --> 01:53:15.029
into the software that
 landed on those workstations.
 And it was very

01:53:15.030 --> 01:53:20.609
much that condition that
 they had absolutely no
 thickness. And the

01:53:20.610 --> 01:53:25.359
ability for them to
 disappear on rotation. That was
 really so much about

01:53:25.360 --> 01:53:30.179
what that time was about.
 But backing up, I just
 want to -- I'm so

01:53:30.180 --> 01:53:34.919
grateful that this event
 is happening. Especially grateful
 that this book

01:53:34.920 --> 01:53:40.119
exists Thank you to Rob,
 David and MIT Press for
 making it happen And

01:53:40.120 --> 01:53:47.499
to the designers. The book
 is so beautiful. And it
 will -- it's such a

01:53:47.500 --> 01:53:53.329
perfect tribute to Muriel
 Cooper's work. I was
 really honored to be

01:53:53.330 --> 01:53:57.549
asked to do the forward.
 And I was given -- I'm
 not surprised -- a really

01:53:57.550 --> 01:54:00.859
impossible deadline to write
 the forward for the
 book. I thought, there's

01:54:00.860 --> 01:54:05.869
no way I will meet
 this deadline. And I did, much
 to my surprise. And the

01:54:05.870 --> 01:54:13.959
words, the anecdotes, the
 experience, really just kind of
 came out of me

01:54:13.960 --> 01:54:18.179
And it was such
 a pleasure. And really
 meaningful right now. I'm

01:54:18.180 --> 01:54:24.329
assuming no one has read
 the forward yet. But in
 many ways, it reads

01:54:24.330 --> 01:54:28.879
as a kind of
 instruction manual for how to
 run a really successful

01:54:28.880 --> 01:54:36.249
research lab. And sort
 of serving my own current
 agenda. Muriel -- part

01:54:36.250 --> 01:54:43.229
of Muriel's brilliance, I think,
 was in her collaborators. And
 I also -- I

01:54:43.230 --> 01:54:47.659
mean, you know, the VLW
 would not have been the
 place it was of

01:54:47.660 --> 01:54:54.429
course without Ron
 and Dave, Suguru. And
 Louis Whitesman (phonetic)

01:54:54.430 --> 01:54:57.459
is here. The group
 of people that she
 carefully curated was really

01:54:57.460 --> 01:55:03.329
extraordinary. So I encourage
 you to actually read
 the whole book. The

01:55:03.330 --> 01:55:08.079
essays are incredible.
 Nicholas' afterwards is incredible
 And again, if

01:55:08.080 --> 01:55:11.269
you want a sense of what
 it was like to be in
 the VLW, please read it

01:55:11.270 --> 01:55:18.559
Just a couple little anecdotes
 to share. And this is
 something that -- so

01:55:18.560 --> 01:55:22.759
maybe we'll have a
 conversation about this at
 some point But Muriel

01:55:22.760 --> 01:55:27.319
forbid technology conversations
 at the VLW. Literally.
 This is something

01:55:27.320 --> 01:55:33.389
that I write about in
 the forward. You could not
 talk about technology in

01:55:33.390 --> 01:55:37.849
front of her She
 would tell you to
 go to another room

01:55:37.850 --> 01:55:40.529
 (Chuckles).

01:55:40.530 --> 01:55:42.609
&gt;&gt; It's true, right?

01:55:42.610 --> 01:55:44.119
 &gt;&gt; Yeah.

01:55:44.120 --> 01:55:46.899
&gt;&gt; And there
 was something incredibly important
 about this. And absolutely

01:55:46.900 --> 01:55:52.029
key to what
 she was able to do in
separating these conversations

01:55:52.030 --> 01:55:55.409
that were
 really about experience, about
 design, about communication,

01:55:55.410 --> 01:56:00.829
about messages, from how
 it was done. She
 never let technology lead.

01:56:00.830 --> 01:56:03.289
I mean,
 we had those
 conversations of course.

01:56:03.290 --> 01:56:06.569
But they did not
 lead. And I write about this
 in the forward. I feel

01:56:06.570 --> 01:56:13.169
like I did bring this
 ethic, what I learned, to
Pentagram, which, of course, now --

01:56:13.170 --> 01:56:17.209
when I joined Pentagram
 I was kind of
 the first digitally fluent

01:56:17.210 --> 01:56:21.119
partner to join. My team
 was very much -- I
 mean, I built my practice

01:56:21.120 --> 01:56:26.219
doing the digital interactive
 work of my partners who
 were doing these --

01:56:26.220 --> 01:56:32.279
still doing these
 amazing identity programs
 for corporate institutional

01:56:32.280 --> 01:56:36.589
clients. But what really
 became strategic, of course, as
 we knew was to

01:56:36.590 --> 01:56:43.919
have that type of work
 be done by all the
 partners for their identity

01:56:43.920 --> 01:56:49.419
programs. And again, you
 should read the forward.
 But when -- years

01:56:49.420 --> 01:56:54.469
ago when people came
 to Pentagram, when I started,
 they asked for a

01:56:54.470 --> 01:56:57.719
certain artifact of design.
 A poster. An Annual Report
 A book. And that

01:56:57.720 --> 01:57:03.719
has completely changed. And
 again, it's something that
 -- so what

01:57:03.720 --> 01:57:10.399
clients now ask for is
 -- we have educated the
 clients to ask for their

01:57:10.400 --> 01:57:16.499
message to be communicated
 to an audience It's
 the work has become

01:57:16.500 --> 01:57:20.779
really media agnostic. Anyways,
 all of these things
 I really took away

01:57:20.780 --> 01:57:28.879
from my experience in the
 VLW. I talk about --
 there were a group of

01:57:28.880 --> 01:57:33.879
students with diverse
 set of backgrounds from
 the computational side,

01:57:33.880 --> 01:57:41.949
architecture, design. The work
 -- Muriel had really guided
 us. She didn't --

01:57:41.950 --> 01:57:47.709
she taught in a very
 unusual way. I didn't even
 know that I was being

01:57:47.710 --> 01:57:54.509
taught exactly. But I
 was really being guided. And
 in hindsight I look

01:57:54.510 --> 01:58:00.969
back. And in some ways the
heaviest hand was really in
the choice of typefaces.

01:58:00.970 --> 01:58:02.879
 (Chuckles).

01:58:02.880 --> 01:58:05.509
&gt;&gt; And there were
 many weights. But there
 was one typeface,

01:58:05.510 --> 01:58:11.239
which was Helvetica. So
 this diversity of work that
 we produced had --

01:58:11.240 --> 01:58:17.329
came together in that way.
 And lastly, I had toe
 write about what --

01:58:17.330 --> 01:58:22.199
I wanted to write
 and sort of conclude
 the forward with

01:58:22.200 --> 01:58:26.739
what I felt really
 motivated her. I think she
 was -- there were

01:58:26.740 --> 01:58:31.899
contradictions in that.
 I distinctly remember
 this conversation with

01:58:31.900 --> 01:58:37.289
either Ron or another
 designer, graphic designer, who
 was visiting. And

01:58:37.290 --> 01:58:40.299
they were -- I
 was just overhearing this
 conversation with Muriel. And

01:58:40.300 --> 01:58:44.469
they were talking about
 the one word that would
 be kind of devastating

01:58:44.470 --> 01:58:47.479
to hear about
 their work. Do
 you remember this?

01:58:47.480 --> 01:58:49.159
&gt;&gt; Oh, yeah.

01:58:49.160 --> 01:58:50.939
&gt;&gt; And do you remember the word?

01:58:50.940 --> 01:58:52.989
&gt;&gt; Oh, yeah. Nice.

01:58:52.990 --> 01:58:54.979
&gt;&gt; Oh, okay

01:58:54.980 --> 01:58:57.469
&gt;&gt; I had a different memory
 of the word. &gt;

01:58:57.470 --> 01:58:59.419
&gt; It didn't matter
 what the word was as
 long as you cried.

01:58:59.420 --> 01:59:01.359
&gt;&gt; Okay. (Chuckles).

01:59:01.360 --> 01:59:05.089
&gt;&gt; The
 word I remember
 was decorative.

01:59:05.090 --> 01:59:06.369
&gt;&gt; Oh, yes.

01:59:06.370 --> 01:59:09.009
&gt;&gt; Similar; similar

01:59:09.010 --> 01:59:11.009
&gt;&gt; So on the
 one hand, Muriel, you know,
 wanted there to be meaning,

01:59:11.010 --> 01:59:16.129
she wanted to communicate.
 She wanted it to map to
 the experience of the content.

01:59:16.130 --> 01:59:18.779
She wanted
 it to represent
 the content.

01:59:18.780 --> 01:59:21.679
She wanted her
 work to represent
 the content On

01:59:21.680 --> 01:59:25.809
the other hand, another memory
 I have is I was
 on the reality engine,

01:59:25.810 --> 01:59:31.409
flying her through a
 financial visualization I had
 done. She actually gave

01:59:31.410 --> 01:59:37.539
me the Morningstar floppy
 disk so I could use
 that information. And I

01:59:37.540 --> 01:59:46.009
remember I was kind of
 driving the demo and she
 was sitting back in

01:59:46.010 --> 01:59:51.309
the chair. And she really
 -- she asked me to
 kind of pause. She really

01:59:51.310 --> 01:59:54.959
wanted to occupy the
 space of numbers. She
 actually -- despite the

01:59:54.960 --> 01:59:59.740
other comment -- this contradiction
is she wanted to feel it, she
wanted to be seduced

02:00:00.000 --> 02:00:04.829
by the form. And I
 think it was really those
 two things of wanting the

02:00:04.830 --> 02:00:09.129
meaning, you know, wanting
 the content. And wanting
 just the seduction

02:00:09.130 --> 02:00:16.349
and the pleasure of
 beautiful form. And flying,
 as Richard said. She

02:00:16.350 --> 02:00:22.629
wanted to fly. So -- and
 lastly, when I tried to sum
 this up in the forward, I

02:00:22.630 --> 02:00:28.479
was writing a sentence
 about what really motivated
 her. There's an M

02:00:28.480 --> 02:00:31.869
dash. I was trying to
 put the words together. And
 I concluded that what

02:00:31.870 --> 02:00:36.059
motivated her
 really was what
 was possible.

02:00:36.060 --> 02:00:37.909
&gt;&gt; Very good.

02:00:37.910 --> 02:00:41.729
&gt;&gt; So thank
 you. And great
 to be here.

02:00:41.730 --> 02:00:46.629
 (Applause).

02:00:46.630 --> 02:00:48.233
&gt;&gt; I think we're running
a little late so we'll

02:00:48.234 --> 02:00:49.769
take just another couple
of minutes David,

02:00:49.770 --> 02:00:54.429
I was going
 to ask you, you said
 outside, you said, boy,

02:00:54.430 --> 02:00:58.379
I'm looking back. And gee,
 I didn't really study with
 Muriel for all that long.

02:00:58.380 --> 02:01:01.019
It was only ten years.

02:01:01.020 --> 02:01:02.749
 (Chuckles).

02:01:02.750 --> 02:01:07.129
&gt; &gt; And my recollection --

02:01:07.130 --> 02:01:09.609
&gt;&gt; It felt so much longer.

02:01:09.610 --> 02:01:11.359
&gt;&gt; But my recollection
 is you changed quite a
 lot over those ten years.

02:01:11.360 --> 02:01:15.119
I thought you could say a
 little bit about what you were like
 when you started with Muriel and --

02:01:15.120 --> 02:01:17.229
&gt;&gt; Yeah. I -- she
 -- I mean, like many
 people, she changed my life.

02:01:17.230 --> 02:01:21.439
But I feel like,
 wow, she really changed the
 trajectory of my life.

02:01:21.440 --> 02:01:28.039
When I started,
 I hadn't written any
 software per se.

02:01:28.040 --> 02:01:32.149
At least not at
 MIT as an undergraduate.
 I had worked in

02:01:32.150 --> 02:01:35.119
the dark room.
 But that was all
 just with chemistry.

02:01:35.120 --> 02:01:38.929
And I didn't really
 know anything about design I
 had finished my undergrad.

02:01:38.930 --> 02:01:42.279
I didn't have a job.
 I didn't know quite what
 I was going to do.

02:01:42.280 --> 02:01:46.329
And I don't know if
 it was she or I. But
 we talked about like, what

02:01:46.330 --> 02:01:51.249
should I do next? She
 says, well, come do work
 in the Visible Language

02:01:51.250 --> 02:01:56.559
Workshop. And you know, thank
 God that I did. So
 I started out like that.

02:01:56.560 --> 02:02:02.489
Bob Sabiston taught me
 all he knew about software.
 And I taught other

02:02:02.490 --> 02:02:06.129
people And we all taught
 each other. None of us --
 a lot of people came

02:02:06.130 --> 02:02:10.199
to the VLW not knowing
 how to program. And none
 of us started by

02:02:10.200 --> 02:02:14.459
taking Computer Science course
 in how to program.
 We taught each

02:02:14.460 --> 02:02:19.159
other. And that was going
 to be enough. And yeah,
 in those ten years,

02:02:19.160 --> 02:02:25.109
everything changed so dramatically
 in the way that I
 had absorbed all of

02:02:25.110 --> 02:02:29.479
this idea about stuff. And I
 never felt like she taught me
 -- like I was being

02:02:29.480 --> 02:02:32.279
taught. I
 kind of complained
 about it sometimes

02:02:32.280 --> 02:02:34.369
 (Chuckles).

02:02:34.370 --> 02:02:36.619
&gt;&gt; Where is the
 textbook? Is there ever going
 to be a textbook?

02:02:36.620 --> 02:02:38.569
Are you even
 working on the
 textbook? No.

02:02:38.570 --> 02:02:41.789
There was no chance there
 was ever going to be
 the textbook to come out.

02:02:41.790 --> 02:02:45.959
Which, you know, a lot
 of people at the lab,
 they had a group.

02:02:45.960 --> 02:02:50.389
And they solely developed
 that book. That was
 not going to happen.

02:02:50.390 --> 02:02:54.349
But it was like this.
 It was like this --
 this is the Bob Sabiston

02:02:54.350 --> 02:02:58.599
view of the
 Visible Language Workshop. And
 it was like tied t

02:02:58.600 --> 02:03:01.499
ogether with duct tape
 and there were a bunch
 of speakers and smoke

02:03:01.500 --> 02:03:06.539
coming out of things. And at
 the end of it, I had
 learned all of this stuff.

02:03:06.540 --> 02:03:10.479
And I didn't even
 quite realize how much
 she was teaching me

02:03:10.480 --> 02:03:12.999
&gt;&gt; Yeah. I have
 two more really quick questions.
 One is for you, Ron.

02:03:13.000 --> 02:03:17.069
I was thinking at
 the end of the day
 -- these last two days

02:03:17.070 --> 02:03:20.319
have been our
 members Sponsor Days at
 the Media Lab.

02:03:20.320 --> 02:03:24.339
At the end of the
 day yesterday after it was
 hectic all day, our newest

02:03:24.340 --> 02:03:29.739
faculty member, Danielle
 Wood, who is a
 remarkable young woman who

02:03:29.740 --> 02:03:33.469
works at
 NASA and also works
 on community activism,

02:03:33.470 --> 02:03:36.019
was walking around looking
 at demos. And she
 came to our group

02:03:36.020 --> 02:03:39.419
and my students were
 all around. She asked --
 she's just starting at the

02:03:39.420 --> 02:03:44.819
Media Lab. And
 she said, what is it
 that makes a group bond?

02:03:44.820 --> 02:03:48.349
What is it
 that makes a group
 feel special? And I

02:03:48.350 --> 02:03:50.849
immediately thought of all of
 the groups I've ever seen
 at the Media Lab.

02:03:50.850 --> 02:03:54.129
There was something
 about the feeling at
 the Visible Language

02:03:54.130 --> 02:03:57.549
Workshop that was just special.
 And I was wondering, you
 know, just in a

02:03:57.550 --> 02:04:02.119
few words, can you
 think how that worked? Why
 that happened? Tell Danielle

02:04:02.120 --> 02:04:12.149
&gt;&gt; Right. Because as you
 saw, which chose superb people. And
 there really was no roadmap.

02:04:12.150 --> 02:04:18.389
There was a work
 space. And some, you know,
 general ideas of things.

02:04:18.390 --> 02:04:25.219
And we all sort
 of dived in. And got
 to work. I mean,

02:04:25.220 --> 02:04:29.429
I don't think
 it was much more
 complicated than that.

02:04:29.430 --> 02:04:33.519
&gt;&gt; My last
 quick question for
 Lisa is,

02:04:33.520 --> 02:04:41.039
you told me before
 that you have a
 daughter. She's 13 you said?

02:04:41.040 --> 02:04:43.869
&gt;&gt; She'll be 10 on Monday.

02:04:43.870 --> 02:04:46.439
&gt;&gt; She'll be
 10. Who is
 interested in design.

02:04:46.440 --> 02:04:50.829
Interested in MIT. And I
 just thought maybe you would tell
 everyone what her name is.

02:04:50.830 --> 02:04:53.669
&gt;&gt; Her name is Muriel.

02:04:53.670 --> 02:04:56.359
&gt;&gt; Thanks.

02:04:56.360 --> 02:05:08.689
 (Applause).

02:05:08.690 --> 02:05:40.959
 [Music].

02:05:40.960 --> 02:05:44.939
&gt;&gt; I'm Muriel's nephew,
 Jonathan. And on behalf
 of Muriel's family, many

02:05:44.940 --> 02:05:50.039
of whom are here tonight,
 a big thank you to the
 Press and to the Media Lab

02:05:50.040 --> 02:05:53.179
for hosting
 and including us in
 this great event.

02:05:53.180 --> 02:05:56.189
Much of the talk
 today has been about
 Muriel's role as a

02:05:56.190 --> 02:05:59.879
teacher and designer. But
 before she was either, she
 was the first born

02:05:59.880 --> 02:06:05.119
child of immigrant Russian
 and Polish parents living
 in Brookline. It was

02:06:05.120 --> 02:06:08.339
a typical middle class
 family Jack, her father,
 was a master electrician.

02:06:08.340 --> 02:06:11.649
And her mother, Jenny, stayed
 at home to take care
 of the family. Muriel

02:06:11.650 --> 02:06:17.049
or Candy as she was
 called, was her nickname, was
 followed by the birth

02:06:17.050 --> 02:06:20.879
of my mother, Helene,
 who is also known as
 Honey. And lastly Charlotte,

02:06:20.880 --> 02:06:25.779
who is here tonight,
 who was known as
 Chicky. Wonderful names.

02:06:25.780 --> 02:06:28.279
 (Chuckles).

02:06:28.280 --> 02:06:31.059
&gt;&gt; Muriel had a pretty
 normal childhood. The only hint
 of what was to

02:06:31.060 --> 02:06:34.009
become a brilliant
 career might have been
 a passion for doodling

02:06:34.010 --> 02:06:37.069
and her being at
 the time what was called
 a tomboy but today

02:06:37.070 --> 02:06:41.919
would simply be
 described as being fearless,
 strong willed and

02:06:41.920 --> 02:06:50.079
independent. When it was time for college, Muriel attend
 Ohio State with the goal of getting her BFA.
 But when Jack and Jenny would visit her on

02:06:50.080 --> 02:06:54.149
Parents Day Weekend, they
 were shocked to discover
 that the dorm that

02:06:54.150 --> 02:06:57.799
Muriel was staying in
 was filled with girls
 of questionable character.

02:06:57.800 --> 02:06:59.669
 (Chuckles).

02:06:59.670 --> 02:07:04.789
&gt;&gt; Who were smoking, leaving
 clothes all over the floor
 and God knows what else.

02:07:04.790 --> 02:07:06.769
In their opinion Ohio
 State was far too racy a
 place for their little girl.

02:07:06.770 --> 02:07:09.789
So Muriel was allowed
 to finish the year and
 then was brought back

02:07:09.790 --> 02:07:13.359
to Boston where
 she enrolled at the
 Massachusetts College of Art

02:07:13.360 --> 02:07:18.339
to pursue a career
 in art education. When I
 applied to art schools

02:07:18.340 --> 02:07:20.949
and was obsessing
 about which art school
 I should attend,

02:07:20.950 --> 02:07:25.539
Muriel calmly told me to
 relax. The school is unimportant.
 It's who you go

02:07:25.540 --> 02:07:30.199
to school with. I can
 see why she felt that
 way. Muriel's classmates at

02:07:30.200 --> 02:07:33.999
Mass Art included a
 who's who of future
 designers and artists, including

02:07:34.000 --> 02:07:38.699
Tom Wong and Jackie
 Casey, both of whom could
 often be found sitting

02:07:38.700 --> 02:07:42.229
around the Cooper dining
 room table, pulling a
 charrette for the next

02:07:42.230 --> 02:07:45.489
morning's class. They
 three became life-long friends.
 Jack and Jenny

02:07:45.490 --> 02:07:49.839
often worried about Muriel
 Why wasn't she married?
 Why didn't she

02:07:49.840 --> 02:07:52.649
conform like her siblings?
 Why did she dress the
 way she dressed? They

02:07:52.650 --> 02:07:56.729
could only see that Muriel
 didn't fit the mold that
 her younger sisters did

02:07:56.730 --> 02:08:01.489
and they didn't see her
 brilliance. Muriel was my aunt.
 But she was much

02:08:01.490 --> 02:08:05.069
more than that to me.
 She was my mentor, my
 idol, and my best friend.

02:08:05.070 --> 02:08:08.719
She was cool. She was
 loud. She could be brash. And
 she was a big part

02:08:08.720 --> 02:08:11.279
of why I became a
 designer. Well, to be honest,
 in large part, it was

02:08:11.280 --> 02:08:14.339
because of her basement.
 Every year would start
 with a call. Muriel

02:08:14.340 --> 02:08:20.089
asking if I would like
 to come by some weekend
 and help clean up her

02:08:20.090 --> 02:08:26.469
basement. And every year
 I leapt at the chance.
 I would spend hours

02:08:26.470 --> 02:08:30.169
upon hours in that
 basement with Muriel asking
 her questions about

02:08:30.170 --> 02:08:33.239
where she had gotten
 this or that object. Where
 she thought a particular

02:08:33.240 --> 02:08:36.549
item should go. And
 she would patiently show
 me how to plan,

02:08:36.550 --> 02:08:41.269
categorize and organize. Looking
 back, I guess it
 was the equivalent to

02:08:41.270 --> 02:08:44.939
Mr. Miyagi's wash on/wax
 off method of karate
 training, except for a

02:08:44.940 --> 02:08:49.329
designer. To me, that
 basement was a playground.
 A library. A time

02:08:49.330 --> 02:08:54.909
capsule. And a toy store
 all rolled into one. And
 I can remember the

02:08:54.910 --> 02:08:58.239
contents of each box
 and shelf like it was
 yesterday. Walking down the

02:08:58.240 --> 02:09:01.569
steep basement stairs, I
 spy a rusty erector set,
 barely contained by it's

02:09:01.570 --> 02:09:05.799
decaying box. It was
 a gift from Muriel's parents
 to keep her occupied

02:09:05.800 --> 02:09:10.119
while she was recovering
 from appendicitis when she
 was 10 years old.

02:09:10.120 --> 02:09:13.769
And Charlotte recently told
 me when Muriel first asked
 for an erector set,

02:09:13.770 --> 02:09:18.109
her mother replied, what on
 earth would a girl do
 with an erector set?

02:09:18.110 --> 02:09:23.909
And great things apparently
 A large wok framed
 by a bamboo steaming

02:09:23.910 --> 02:09:27.169
basket sits on a
 shelf, part of a collection
 of kitchen gadgets, a

02:09:27.170 --> 02:09:31.229
byproduct of Muriel's
 adventures in Chinese cooking.
 My brother, David,

02:09:31.230 --> 02:09:34.979
lived at Muriel's house
 while he attended BU in
 the '60s and remembers

02:09:34.980 --> 02:09:38.449
going with Muriel to
 Chinatown where she would spend
 half of her time

02:09:38.450 --> 02:09:42.039
purchasing exotic ingredients
 with barely pronounceable
 names and the

02:09:42.040 --> 02:09:45.919
other half chatting with
 the store owners. Muriel
 loved exploring world

02:09:45.920 --> 02:09:49.219
cuisines long before it
 became commonplace. And one
 section of the

02:09:49.220 --> 02:09:54.809
basement is filled with
 clothes. A few from
 India with fantastic colors

02:09:54.810 --> 02:09:59.319
and patterns. Some from
 Japan. Rich in their
 simplicity and detail. And

02:09:59.320 --> 02:10:05.759
many, many, many from
 Filene's Basement. I think it
 was Elise Brink, a

02:10:05.760 --> 02:10:08.729
good friend of Muriel's, who
 told the story of how she
 joined in on one of

02:10:08.730 --> 02:10:12.229
Muriel's famous trips to
 Filene's. Muriel went up to
 the returns desk and

02:10:12.230 --> 02:10:14.979
placed a very large pile
 of clothes on the counter.
 And the woman at the

02:10:14.980 --> 02:10:20.369
counter slowly looked through
 the pile and shook
 her head and

02:10:20.370 --> 02:10:23.739
explained, they couldn't
 explain the return. The
 clothes had been

02:10:23.740 --> 02:10:27.749
purchased over a year ago.
 It was too late to
 return them. And without

02:10:27.750 --> 02:10:31.389
missing a beat, Muriel
 calmly explained the reason
 she hadn't returned

02:10:31.390 --> 02:10:34.069
them sooner was because
 she had been locked
 away in a mental

02:10:34.070 --> 02:10:37.759
institution for the past
 year and she had
 just been released. They

02:10:37.760 --> 02:10:40.749
nervously
 accepted her
 return.

02:10:40.750 --> 02:10:43.559
 (Chuckles).

02:10:43.560 --> 02:10:47.539
&gt;&gt; There are
 watches, some analogue and some
 digital. Most are broken,

02:10:47.540 --> 02:10:51.159
which might have been
 a factor in her
 seeming inability to arrive anywhere

02:10:51.160 --> 02:10:53.529
on time. Large
 piles of parking tickets
 held together by rubber bands.

02:10:53.530 --> 02:10:56.449
A souvenir of
 her apparent refusal to
 ever pay for parking

02:10:56.450 --> 02:11:00.199
. Causing the Cambridge
 police to show up
 at her office at MIT

02:11:00.200 --> 02:11:03.889
and threaten to cuff
 and arrest her on the
 spot in order to get her

02:11:03.890 --> 02:11:07.719
to pay them.
 And there are family
 photographs, magazines and

02:11:07.720 --> 02:11:11.119
clippings. One clipping
 features a photograph
 of Muriel standing

02:11:11.120 --> 02:11:15.599
in an all-white
 room and the headline
 states: Designer boldly

02:11:15.600 --> 02:11:20.479
paints home
 interior white.
 (Chuckles).

02:11:20.480 --> 02:11:23.149
&gt;&gt; The article
 goes onto describe
 how Muriel Cooper

02:11:23.150 --> 02:11:25.829
of Brookline
 in a radical move
 breaking with tradition has

02:11:25.830 --> 02:11:29.259
decided to paint the
 interior of her house completely
 white. I know, crazy.

02:11:29.260 --> 02:11:35.579
On the floor sits
 an unopened pack of cigarettes.
 As I bend down

02:11:35.580 --> 02:11:38.659
to pick them up, I
 get a clear image of
 Muriel's habit of approaching a

02:11:38.660 --> 02:11:42.479
random smoker. A total
 stranger. Asking if she
 might smell their

02:11:42.480 --> 02:11:45.809
cigarette. She would hold
 it in her hands
 delicately like a wine

02:11:45.810 --> 02:11:50.379
connoisseur might hold the
 cork of a fine
 vintage wine. Deeply inhaling

02:11:50.380 --> 02:11:55.069
from the flowing tip. A
 look of ecstasy spreading over
 her face. I've quit

02:11:55.070 --> 02:11:59.099
smoking, she would
 explain. But God, I
 miss the smell

02:11:59.100 --> 02:12:01.379
 (Chuckles).

02:12:01.380 --> 02:12:04.239
&gt;&gt; There's a box of
 calculators on the floor. A
 souvenir of a calculus

02:12:04.240 --> 02:12:07.749
class. One of
 many that Muriel
 audited at MIT.

02:12:07.750 --> 02:12:10.649
I remember how
 frustrated she would get
 doing problem sets.

02:12:10.650 --> 02:12:13.539
Often cursing and
 swearing outloud. And how
 at the time

02:12:13.540 --> 02:12:17.529
I wondered why
 anyone would take a
 math class unless

02:12:17.530 --> 02:12:23.279
they absolutely had to. There
 are cameras, lots and lots
 of cameras. 35 millimeter,

02:12:23.280 --> 02:12:28.719
4 by 4 And
 larger slide projectors, movie
 cameras. 16 millimeter,

02:12:28.720 --> 02:12:32.369
8 millimeter. Super
 8. And of course
 Polaroid cameras A

02:12:32.370 --> 02:12:36.639
medium that she loved. Right
 next to them is what
 can only be described

02:12:36.640 --> 02:12:40.949
as a shrine
 to all things Sony.
 Recorders, microphones, headphones,

02:12:40.950 --> 02:12:44.859
Walkman, Betamax cameras
 and players, laser
 disks, editing equipment.

02:12:44.860 --> 02:12:50.409
I realize that I'm
 looking at the complete history
 of Sony Corporation up

02:12:50.410 --> 02:12:57.399
to 1994 as curated by
 Muriel Cooper. There are toys
 on a shelf. Mostly

02:12:57.400 --> 02:13:01.929
robots from Japan. Her
 latest fascination. And they
 trigger a memory of

02:13:01.930 --> 02:13:05.549
the elaborate Viking ship Muriel
 gave me when I was
 5 or so, complete

02:13:05.550 --> 02:13:09.679
with articulated oars that
 would row in sync as
 it slowly moved sails

02:13:09.680 --> 02:13:13.699
billowing across the floor. It
 was and continues to be
 the best present I

02:13:13.700 --> 02:13:17.679
ever got. But most of
 the basement is taken up
 by books. And I take

02:13:17.680 --> 02:13:21.399
frequent breaks having picked up
 a book to place it
 on a shelf only to

02:13:21.400 --> 02:13:26.969
stop, sit on the
 floor, and leaf through its
 pages. Bauhaus, "Learning from

02:13:26.970 --> 02:13:31.199
Las Vegas", filed under
 architecture, the whole earth
 catalog, books by

02:13:31.200 --> 02:13:35.529
Paul Rand Josef
 Albers, Push Pin magazine,
 upper case/lower case

02:13:35.530 --> 02:13:38.319
comic books. Not even
 reading. Just absorbing the
 pacing, the structure,

02:13:38.320 --> 02:13:43.019
the color, the visual
 impact that each page
 contained. And I was

02:13:43.020 --> 02:13:46.829
mesmerized by the cover
 designs, the fonts and the
 images. And at the

02:13:46.830 --> 02:13:51.739
time I didn't even realize
 her connection to these books
 that I was falling

02:13:51.740 --> 02:13:54.699
in love with. Every item
 in that basement was precious
 to Muriel. If I

02:13:54.700 --> 02:13:57.509
asked why I couldn't throw
 out a calculator, a Betamax
 player, or a gear

02:13:57.510 --> 02:14:01.519
bag, all of them
 long since broken or obsolete,
 Muriel would comment on

02:14:01.520 --> 02:14:07.709
how the interface, packaging
 or design was cute.
 And cute was the

02:14:07.710 --> 02:14:12.129
highest form of flattery
 that Muriel could bestow on
 an object or idea.

02:14:12.130 --> 02:14:15.119
We spent many weekends
 together at the flea
 market in Squantum,

02:14:15.120 --> 02:14:20.089
coming upon a vendor
 selling an interesting old
 sees or piece of

02:14:20.090 --> 02:14:23.109
equipment. Muriel would
 immediately start a
 conversation with the

02:14:23.110 --> 02:14:26.149
seller. She was not only
 curious about the story behind
 an object, but the

02:14:26.150 --> 02:14:30.869
story of the owner, as
 well. And after ten minutes
 or so of chatting, she

02:14:30.870 --> 02:14:37.609
would stop, pause, look
 out over her reading
 glasses and remark, "Cute."

02:14:37.610 --> 02:14:41.469
And the poor vendor
 would look so confused Was
 cute meant as a

02:14:41.470 --> 02:14:43.719
putdown? Should they
 be offended or
 complicated? They didn't

02:14:43.720 --> 02:14:46.669
understand that coming
 from Muriel cute was
 the highest of

02:14:46.670 --> 02:14:51.529
compliments. I was with
 Muriel when she died
 in Mass General Hospital

02:14:51.530 --> 02:14:55.649
in May of 1994. And I
 can honestly say, like many of
 you, not a day has

02:14:55.650 --> 02:15:00.049
gone by that I haven't
 thought about her. I'm not
 a religious man. But I've

02:15:00.050 --> 02:15:04.339
always liked the song Rock
 and Roll Heaven, which is
 goes, if there's a

02:15:04.340 --> 02:15:08.479
Rock and Roll Heaven, well,
 you know they've got a
 hell of a band. So

02:15:08.480 --> 02:15:12.159
who knows? Maybe there's
 a graphic design heaven.
 And Jackie, Tom

02:15:12.160 --> 02:15:17.109
and Muriel are in
 a white cloud-filled room
 sitting around the table,

02:15:17.110 --> 02:15:19.769
laughing and drinking wine.
 They are solving design
 problems we can't

02:15:19.770 --> 02:15:23.279
possibly imagine. It's
 just another day of
 a perfect never-ending

02:15:23.280 --> 02:15:28.429
charrette. Then suddenly at
 this very moment, Muriel
 pauses, gets that

02:15:28.430 --> 02:15:34.079
twinkle in her eye,
 peers out over her reading
 glasses, and comments on

02:15:34.080 --> 02:15:39.189
how cute our
 celebration here today has
 been. Thank you.

02:15:39.190 --> 02:15:55.629
 (Applause).

02:15:55.630 --> 02:16:00.519
&gt;&gt; Two more short speakers. And I
 would like to thank you for coming.
 You will not see me again.

02:16:00.520 --> 02:16:12.259
And Joi Ito is our next
 speaker And Amy Brand, who is
 the head of the MIT Press

02:16:12.260 --> 02:16:18.829
is our last speaker.
 If a parent can give their
 child to a foster home

02:16:18.830 --> 02:16:26.189
or to somebody else and
 be really happy with where
 that child is placed,

02:16:26.190 --> 02:16:31.769
I, in some sense, gave
 my child to Joi Ito
 who has been the director

02:16:31.770 --> 02:16:35.209
of the Media Lab
 for he reminded me
 yesterday almost six years.

02:16:35.210 --> 02:16:40.989
I think of it
 as yesterday. And when Joi joined,
 it was the best thing

02:16:40.990 --> 02:16:48.019
that happened to this place. So
 Joi, you didn't know Muriel. But
 you're cut from the same cloth

02:16:48.020 --> 02:16:55.179
 (Applause)

02:16:55.180 --> 02:16:57.389
&gt;&gt; Thank you, everybody.
And I really appreciate
everybody coming together

02:16:57.390 --> 02:17:03.149
to celebrate Muriel
 And it's interesting. It's
 not the happiest thing.

02:17:03.150 --> 02:17:08.959
But we have
 recently lost Seymour and Marvin
 and this feels to me

02:17:08.960 --> 02:17:12.869
like a kind
 of culmination of this
 sort of historical retrospective

02:17:12.870 --> 02:17:18.409
of the founding
 faculty of the Media
 Lab. And I think it's,

02:17:18.410 --> 02:17:23.829
for me, in addition
 to hearing about all of
 their work through students

02:17:23.830 --> 02:17:27.059
and faculty, through
 Nicholas, meeting all
 of the people

02:17:27.060 --> 02:17:30.429
who were connected to
 them and listening to
 the conversations really

02:17:30.430 --> 02:17:33.169
helps me understand where a
 lot of this stuff comes
 from And this kind

02:17:33.170 --> 02:17:38.559
of very strong creativity,
 disobedience, and this sort
 of fearlessness that

02:17:38.560 --> 02:17:44.129
sort of embodies the
 Media Lab. And I've known
 Nicholas for a long

02:17:44.130 --> 02:17:47.729
time. But as I start
 to see these other people
 who are involved, I'm

02:17:47.730 --> 02:17:51.829
starting to understand more
 and more about the DNA.
 And it's I think

02:17:51.830 --> 02:17:58.959
quite a testament to the
 founding faculty at the Media
 Lab that 31 years

02:17:58.960 --> 02:18:04.849
now later, you can still feel
 the DNA. And I think that
 this -- the fact that

02:18:04.850 --> 02:18:11.119
we're still on a trajectory
 that was set by the
 original founders and set so

02:18:11.120 --> 02:18:16.769
strongly is a testament
 to both their vision and
 their character. So today

02:18:16.770 --> 02:18:20.959
has been really wonderful
 in sort of understanding
 that. I'm peculiarly

02:18:20.960 --> 02:18:25.929
sort of overlapping with Muriel
 on that I'm also on
 the Board of the MIT

02:18:25.930 --> 02:18:30.429
Press. I'm an author there
 and also the director of
 the Media Lab. And I

02:18:30.430 --> 02:18:34.109
think Muriel played a
 very important role in bringing
 the Press and the

02:18:34.110 --> 02:18:38.799
Media Lab together. And
 we're also continuing that. And
 I think there's a

02:18:38.800 --> 02:18:43.309
demonstration in the auditorium.
 But we've launched a
 journal called the

02:18:43.310 --> 02:18:48.099
Journal of Design and Science.
 So a lot of art
 -- Neri has a wonderful

02:18:48.100 --> 02:18:52.319
CREB cycle that I think
 I got from John Maeda, who
 got it from Rich Gold.

02:18:52.320 --> 02:18:57.789
But there's sort of
 four quadrants where you have
 art and science and

02:18:57.790 --> 02:19:02.009
design and engineering And
 we've done a lot of
 art and engineering at

02:19:02.010 --> 02:19:05.769
the Media Lab but the
 sort of design and science
 axis hadn't been fully

02:19:05.770 --> 02:19:09.929
explored in my view so
 we created a journal and
 the idea was that we

02:19:09.930 --> 02:19:13.429
brought a lot of hard
 scientists into the Media Lab.
 And you realize when

02:19:13.430 --> 02:19:16.659
you meet hard scientists is
 that they tend to be
 very focused and not

02:19:16.660 --> 02:19:20.259
very aware of the
 context about the sensibilities
that you need when you

02:19:20.260 --> 02:19:24.669
think about things. And
 not really responsible necessarily
 for the overall

02:19:24.670 --> 02:19:30.129
possibility of the stuff
 that they are interacting with.
 And conversely, a lot

02:19:30.130 --> 02:19:34.959
of the designers we have
 talked to, or I've talked
 to especially when we

02:19:34.960 --> 02:19:39.099
look at MIT, we have
 hundreds of classes that have
 the word design. But

02:19:39.100 --> 02:19:43.259
sort of design --
 Muriel has sort of morphed
 into systems design and

02:19:43.260 --> 02:19:46.709
design of business practices.
 And design of all kinds
 of things. And it's

02:19:46.710 --> 02:19:50.719
become very engineery. And
 there are even sort of
 moves to sort of

02:19:50.720 --> 02:19:54.779
remove the aesthetic side
 of design. And one of
 the things that I've

02:19:54.780 --> 02:19:57.819
noticed as we look
 at complex self-adaptive systems
 like the climate,

02:19:57.820 --> 02:20:04.169
like the human body,
 that what's missing is the
 kind of sensibility. It's

02:20:04.170 --> 02:20:07.869
you're doing things without
 this approach of trying
 to make the thing

02:20:07.870 --> 02:20:11.719
work in a wonderful way.
 And that, in fact, the
 future of design is

02:20:11.720 --> 02:20:16.376
probably going back to a
sensibilityand aesthetics,
a kind of vision that

02:20:16.377 --> 02:20:20.969
I think goes back to where we were
starting. So to me, bringing design

02:20:20.970 --> 02:20:25.919
and science together, bringing
 a new science into
 design, bringing design

02:20:25.920 --> 02:20:29.729
into science is really
 important. I think that's very
 much Media Lab, very

02:20:29.730 --> 02:20:34.409
much MIT, very much
 Muriel. So that's the journal
 that we launched. And

02:20:34.410 --> 02:20:38.159
we're launching it also
 on a platform called
 PubPub. Which was

02:20:38.160 --> 02:20:41.619
developed at the Media
 Lab. And it's a weird
 platform that allows people

02:20:41.620 --> 02:20:44.919
to make comments. It
 allows you to version, track
 the versions. It's very

02:20:44.920 --> 02:20:49.419
much a sort of living
 document of -- and creates sort
 of a new way of

02:20:49.420 --> 02:20:52.729
thinking about peer review
 so it's much more
 participatory. So we would

02:20:52.730 --> 02:20:56.969
love you to take a look
 and tell us what you think.
 And I think -- let's see

02:20:56.970 --> 02:21:02.579
what else I'm supposed to
 talk about. Yeah, and I
 think the main thing is

02:21:02.580 --> 02:21:08.669
that, you know, as we
 sort of embark on this
 next journey, thinking about

02:21:08.670 --> 02:21:13.189
how the work that Muriel
 has done and the work
 that Amy Brand, who

02:21:13.190 --> 02:21:15.609
you'll meet, who is the
 new director of MIT Press,
 how we can move this

02:21:15.610 --> 02:21:19.029
forward is one of the
 core missions of the Media
 Lab. So I really

02:21:19.030 --> 02:21:24.269
appreciate your past support
 and your future support. And
 with that -- oh,

02:21:24.270 --> 02:21:29.369
one last thing I'm supposed to
 say is that there's going to
 be in that room --

02:21:29.370 --> 02:21:34.039
in the Alex Dreyfoos
 Auditorium there will be a
 video of Muriel's TED5

02:21:34.040 --> 02:21:37.179
talk from 1994. So please
 take a look at that
 during the reception. So

02:21:37.180 --> 02:21:41.009
with that, I would like
 to ask Amy Brand, the
 director of MIT Press to

02:21:41.010 --> 02:21:45.729
come up. Thank you.

02:21:45.730 --> 02:21:50.229
 (Applause).

02:21:50.230 --> 02:21:55.359
&gt;&gt; I just think
 this has been a wonderful
 occasion and thank you,

02:21:55.360 --> 02:21:59.829
Joi, to mark this
 enduring partnership between
the Press and the Media Lab.

02:21:59.830 --> 02:22:03.039
The way that Joi
 was talking about the
 Media Lab and the sort

02:22:03.040 --> 02:22:09.359
of quadrant or
 nexus of design and
 science and art and technology

02:22:09.360 --> 02:22:12.439
is very much
 the DNA of the
 MIT Press, as well.

02:22:12.440 --> 02:22:16.019
And I think a lot
 of that does go back
 to Muriel. So just 30

02:22:16.020 --> 02:22:20.259
seconds for me. Lots of
 thank yous. I wish to
 thank all of our amazing

02:22:20.260 --> 02:22:24.739
speakers and panelists and
 in particular, I really
 want to thank Nicholas

02:22:24.740 --> 02:22:30.799
for co-hosting this event.
 I know that it's really,
 really heartfelt for you,

02:22:30.800 --> 02:22:34.729
Nicholas. And I appreciate
 that, given your deep
 friendship. And I only

02:22:34.730 --> 02:22:37.639
wish I had known
 Muriel, too. One person that
 we haven't mentioned this

02:22:37.640 --> 02:22:41.659
evening but really needs
 to be thanked is Roger
 Conover, who is sitting

02:22:41.660 --> 02:22:45.879
right over there. Roger
 is celebrating 40 years as
 an art and architecture

02:22:45.880 --> 02:22:49.729
editor at the MIT
 Press. And he's created
 this incredible legacy around

02:22:49.730 --> 02:22:54.249
publishing and art and
 architecture and design So
 please talk to him.

02:22:54.250 --> 02:23:00.069
 (Applause).

02:23:00.070 --> 02:23:04.469
&gt;&gt; Now, you might
 have thought listening to
 John Maeda that you're actually

02:23:04.470 --> 02:23:10.139
going to have to
 buy the book. But in
 fact, due to a generous

02:23:10.140 --> 02:23:13.829
gift from the School
 of Architecture and planning
 and Hashim Sarkis,

02:23:13.830 --> 02:23:18.939
everybody who was invited to
 this event will leave with
 a copy of the book.

02:23:18.940 --> 02:23:24.579
So . . . I would
 like to thank -- yes. That's
 up there. So thank you, Hashim.

02:23:24.580 --> 02:23:27.069
 (Applause).

02:23:27.070 --> 02:23:30.069
&gt;&gt; But please, if you
 have more people you want
 to buy books for,

02:23:30.070 --> 02:23:33.489
they are there for purchase,
 as well. And Pentagram, we're
 just in awe of you.

02:23:33.490 --> 02:23:38.369
And in particular, again, I
 would like to thank Aron
 Fay and Daisy Lee,

02:23:38.370 --> 02:23:43.509
who provided those
 really gorgeous animations.
 We're definitely

02:23:43.510 --> 02:23:47.229
going to try to make use
 of them at the MIT presence
 the one at the end.

02:23:47.230 --> 02:23:52.489
Many people at the
 Press and the Lab were
 involved in making this

02:23:52.490 --> 02:23:57.209
event possible. I can't try
 to list all of them.
 But there really are three heroes

02:23:57.210 --> 02:24:04.289
of the evening. They
 are Ellen Hoffman and Kate
 Shanaghan and Kate Strauss,

02:24:04.290 --> 02:24:08.029
a really incredible
 and tireless event
 planning trio.

02:24:08.030 --> 02:24:12.309
They have impeccable
 taste. They have
 great humor. And

02:24:12.310 --> 02:24:16.679
they made me involved in
 planning this event a real
 joy and privilege. So

02:24:16.680 --> 02:24:24.169
thank you to all of them.

02:24:24.170 --> 02:24:26.319
 (Applause)

02:24:26.320 --> 02:24:31.879
&gt;&gt; And thanks to all
 of you for coming. And
 please go and enjoy the reception.

02:24:31.880 --> 02:24:35.759
 Thank you.

02:24:35.760 --> 02:24:37.629
 (Applause)

